A queue in C++ programming is a particular form of data configuration structured linearly. Following a specific order in which operations are performed, queues utilize the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) methodology. When removing components from a queue, the first component introduced will be the first to be extracted. 

The queue is included in the Standard Template Library (STL). If you are using queue 'q', it clears out differently than other linear data configurations. This is because queue does not hold a direct member functions to clear the queue. However, you can still remove all elements from the queue by repeatedly calling the pop() function until it is empty. 

Below is the C++ code to eradicate every single component from queue 'q':

```c++
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

// function to clear queue 
void clearQueue(queue<int>& q) 
{ 
   queue<int> empty; 
   swap(q, empty); 
} 

int main() 
{ 
   queue<int> q; 
   q.push(5); 
   q.push(10); 
   q.push(15); 

   clearQueue(q); 

   return 0;
} 
```

This code initially creates a queue 'q' and populates it with the integer elements 5, 10, and 15. We then call the 'clearQueue' function to remove all of these elements. After running this code, the queue 'q' should be empty.

Note: Not necessary to create a new empty queue (called empty in the example) and swap it with the existing one. That approach essentially performs the same action as repeatedly calling the pop() function, but elegantly and in fewer lines of code. However, `while (!q.empty()) q.pop();` works just fine, too. But one must be careful with calling q.pop() when the queue is already empty. It should always be paired with a q.empty() check, as trying to pop an empty queue is undefined behavior.

