Here is a multi-threaded algorithm in C++ using std::thread and std::vector to handle dynamic arrays. The comparison against a single threaded linear search is also done within the code.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>

// This function represents the linear search performed by each thread.
void linear_search(std::vector<int>::iterator begin, std::vector<int>::iterator end, int id, int searchFor, std::vector<int>& results) {
    for(auto it = begin; it != end; ++it)
       if(*it == searchFor) 
           results[id] = 1;
}

int main(void) {
    // Generate an array of size n with random values.
    int n = 100000000;                                         
    std::vector<int> arr(n, 0);                             
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) 
       arr[i] = rand() % n;

    int searchFor = rand() % n;

    // Multi-threaded linear search.
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    int num_threads = std::thread::hardware_concurrency();   // default number of threads.
    std::vector<int> results(num_threads, 0);
    std::vector<std::thread> threads(num_threads);
    int block_size = n / num_threads;
    for(int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i) 
        threads[i] = std::thread(linear_search, arr.begin() + block_size * i, 
                                 (i == num_threads - 1 ? arr.end() : arr.begin() + block_size * (i + 1)), 
                                 i, searchFor, std::ref(results));
    for(auto& thread : threads) thread.join(); // wait for all threads to finish.
    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::cout << "[Multi-threaded] Search took " << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(end - start).count() << " microseconds.\n";

    // Single-threaded linear search for comparison.
    start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    for(auto& num : arr) 
        if(num == searchFor) 
            break;
    end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::cout << "[Single-threaded] Search took " << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(end - start).count() << " microseconds.\n";

    return 0;
}
```

This script creates a large dynamic array at runtime, populates it with random numbers, and then conducts a multi-threaded linear search using std::thread. The same search is then done using a single thread, and the two are compared using a timer to determine which method is more efficient.

Note: This implementation assumes that you're searching for just the first occurrence of the target value. If you need to find all the occurrences, you need to modify the linear_search function to keep checking even after finding an occurrence.

