Implementing a Singleton class with thread-safe instantiation in C++ can be done as follows:

```C++
#include <mutex>

class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton* instance;
    static std::mutex mtx;

    // Private constructor so that no objects can be created.
    Singleton() {}

public:

    // Delete copy constructor and assignment operator
    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        if (instance == nullptr) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            if (instance == nullptr) {
                instance = new Singleton();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
};

// Initialize static members
Singleton* Singleton::instance = nullptr;
std::mutex Singleton::mtx;

int main()
{
    Singleton* s = Singleton::getInstance();
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we use a double-checked locking pattern to ensure that the Singleton instance is only created once, even in a multithreaded environment. We do this by first checking if an instance already exists. If it does not, we lock the mutex to prevent other threads from creating an instance at the same time. Then we check again if an instance was created since our last check. If no instance was created, we create one. 

The `std::lock_guard` is a class template that provides a convenient RAII-style mechanism for owning a mutex for the duration of a scoped block.

The copy constructor and the assignment operator are marked as delete to ensure the Singleton instance cannot be copied.

Creating custom performance counters in unmanaged Visual C++ involves accessing Windows' built-in Performance Data Helper (PDH) API. Below is a simple example to demonstrate how you can create your own performance counter:

Before you proceed with this, make sure that you have included the required libraries (`pdh.lib`) and header files (`pdh.h`) in your project.

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <pdh.h>
#include <pdhmsg.h>

// Define the counter
#define COUNTER_PATH "\\Your Application's Custom Counter\\Your Counter"

void UpdatePerformanceCounter(LONG lValue)
{
  PDH_STATUS Status;
  PDH_HQUERY hQuery = NULL;
  PDH_HCOUNTER hCounter = NULL;

  // Open a query object.
  Status = PdhOpenQuery(NULL, 0, &hQuery);

  if (ERROR_SUCCESS == Status)
  {
    // Add a counter.
    Status = PdhAddCounter(hQuery, COUNTER_PATH, 0, &hCounter);

    if (ERROR_SUCCESS == Status)
    {
      // Collect query data.
      Status = PdhCollectQueryData(hQuery);

      if (ERROR_SUCCESS == Status)
      {
        // Now you can increment the counter
        PDH_FMT_COUNTERVALUE DisplayValue;
        Status = PdhFormatFromRawValue(PDH_FMT_LONG, NULL, NULL, &lValue, NULL, &DisplayValue);

        if (ERROR_SUCCESS == Status)
        {
          // DisplayValue.longValue now contains your counter value
        }

        // Remove the counter after you're done.
        PdhRemoveCounter(hCounter);
      }
    } 

    // Close the query object.
    PdhCloseQuery(hQuery);
  }
}
```

Remember to handle error statuses and cleanup any open handles in an appropriate manner.

Please note, creating a custom performance counter is only half the story. You also need to properly register your custom performance counter with Windows, by creating the appropriate performance registry entries under `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services`.

You should refer to the MSDN documentation on "Creating a Performance Extension DLL" for further details, and how to create the registry entries:
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/perfctrs/creating-a-performance-extension-dll

