```cpp
#include <stdio.h>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

bool palindrome(vector<int> l) {
    int start = 0;
    int end = l.size() - 1;

    // The loop will run until it has checked all pairs of corresponding elements from start and end
    while (start < end) {
        // If there is a pair of elements that don't match, then it's not a palindrome
        if (l[start] != l[end]) {
            return false;
        }

        // Move pointers closer to the center of the vector
        start++;
        end--;
    }

    // If the function hasn't returned false by this point, then it must be a palindrome
    return true;
}
```

This function works by comparing pairs of elements from the start and end of the vector. If all pairs of elements are equal, then the list of numbers is a palindrome. If it finds a pair of elements that don't match, it immediately returns false, signaling that the list isn't a palindrome. If it finishes checking all pairs of elements and hasn't returned false, then it returns true, indicating that the list is a palindrome.

