Compose a C++ code to calculate the sum of all the perfect numbers within range of two specified input values. Condition is that, your program must also identify and correct any potential bugs present in the given erroneous code sample. Your program should further optimize the calculation process with time complexity considerations. 

```C++
#include<iostream> 
using namespace std; 

bool checkPerfect(int n) 
{ 
    int sum = 1; 
    for (int i=2; i*i<=n; i++) 
    { 
        if (n%i==0) 
        { 
            if(i*i!=n) 
                sum = sum + i + n/i; 
            else
                sum = sum + i; 
        } 
    } 
    if (sum == n && n != 1) return true; 

    return false; 
}

int findSum(int low, int high) 
{ 
    int sum = 0; 
    for (int i = low>2? low:2; i <= high; i++) {
        if (checkPerfect(i)) sum += i; 
    }
    return sum; 
} 

int main() 
{ 
    int low = 6; 
    int high = 48; 

    cout << findSum(low, high); 
    return 0; 
}
```

a:
The provided C++ code correctly identifies all the perfect numbers within a specified range and sums them up as required. However, there are a few small areas where we can optimize it. 

1) In the function `checkPerfect(int n)`, it is checking the divisor from 2 to square root of n. But if the number n is 2, the condition in for loop becomes i*i<=2, which means the loop breaks immediately. So, it never goes inside the loop. 

2) In the function `findSum(int low, int high)`, it is checking each number from low to high whether that number is perfect or not, which is unnecessary because after 8128, the next perfect number is 33550336. So you can hard code the perfect numbers for better performance when we have large numbers.

The corrected and optimized C++ code is as follows:

```C++
#include<iostream> 
using namespace std; 

bool checkPerfect(int n) 
{ 
    if(n == 2) return false;

    int sum = 1; 
    for (int i=2; i*i<n; i++) 
    { 
        if (n%i==0) 
        { 
            if(i*i!=n) 
                sum = sum + i + n/i; 
            else
                sum = sum + i; 
        } 
    } 
    if (sum == n && n != 1) return true; 
    return false; 
} 

int findSum(int low, int high) 
{ 
    int sum = 0; 
    int perfectNumbers[] = {6, 28, 496, 8128, 33550336};
    int length = 5;
    for (int i=0; i<length; i++) 
    {
        if (perfectNumbers[i]>=low && perfectNumbers[i]<=high)
            sum += perfectNumbers[i];
    }
    return sum; 
} 

int main() 
{ 
    int low = 1; 
    int high = 50000; 

    cout << findSum(low, high); 
    return 0; 
}
```
Remember, the time complexity of this optimized code still remains O(√n) for the checkPerfect function, but we have greatly reduced the number of times it is called in findSum function, hence improving performance.

