a:
No, the memory is not automatically freed when the DLL is unloaded. 

When you allocate memory (using malloc in your example) within a DLL, the memory is allocated from the heap of the process that loaded the DLL, not from the DLL's own memory space.

Consequently, unloading the DLL doesn't automatically free this allocated memory. The memory leak still exists and the 1k memory will not be available until it's freed using free() or the host process ends.

This is true whether CRT is statically or dynamically linked. The responsibility of freeing memory always lies with the code that allocated it in the first place. 

So in this case, a proper way to handle this memory management is to provide a function in the DLL to deallocate the memory before unloading the DLL, like:

```cpp
void cleanup() {
    free(a);
}
```

And call this function before unloading the DLL:

```cpp
dll->cleanup();
UnloadDLL(dll);
```

