#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int arr1[] = {12, 4, 17, 6, 21, 45, 33};
    int arr2[] = {9, 21, 47, 33, 4};

    int size1 = sizeof(arr1) / sizeof(arr1[0]);
    int size2 = sizeof(arr2) / sizeof(arr2[0]);

    for(int i = 0; i < size1; i++){
        for(int j = 0; j < size2; j++){
            if(arr1[i] = arr2[j]) 
                cout<< arr1[i] << " ";
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Keep in mind that your solution should be optimal in terms of time complexity. Analyze the time complexity of your solution.

a:
The incorrect part of the preliminary code is that it uses `=` instead of `==` for comparison in the `if` statement. The `=` operator in C++ is an assignment operator - it doesn't perform the comparison check. The `==` operator is needed to check if two values are equal.

A more elegant and optimal solution involves utilizing the `set_intersection` algorithm present in `<algorithm>` STL.

Here's the corrected code with optimisations:

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<algorithm>
#include<vector>
using namespace std;

int main(){
    int arr1[] = {12, 4, 17, 6, 21, 45, 33};
    int arr2[] = {9, 21, 47, 33, 4};

    int size1 = sizeof(arr1) / sizeof(arr1[0]);
    int size2 = sizeof(arr2) / sizeof(arr2[0]);

    // Sort both arrays because set_intersection requires sorted ranges
    sort(arr1, arr1+size1);
    sort(arr2, arr2+size2);

    vector<int> result;

    // Using set_intersection algorithm to find common elements
    set_intersection(arr1, arr1+size1, arr2, arr2+size2, back_inserter(result));

    for(int i = 0; i<result.size(); i++)
    {
        cout << result[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The time complexity is O(size1*log(size1) + size2*log(size2)) for sorting both arrays and O(size1 + size2) for the set_intersection call, where size1 and size2 are sizes of input arrays. Therefore, the total time complexity is O(size1*log(size1) + size2*log(size2)) which is more optimal than original implementation having O(size1*size2) complexity.

