Say I have:


void Render(void(*Call)())
{
    D3dDevice->BeginScene();
    Call();
    D3dDevice->EndScene();
    D3dDevice->Present(0,0,0,0);
}



This is fine as long as the function I want to use to render is a function or a `static` member function:


Render(MainMenuRender);
Render(MainMenu::Render);



However, I really want to be able to use a class method as well since in most cases the rendering function will want to access member variables, and Id rather not make the class instance global, e.g.


Render(MainMenu->Render);



However I really have no idea how to do this, and still allow functions and `static` member functions to be used.

a:
You can't take the address of a member function in the same way you can a normal function - they're not exactly the same. This is because member functions implicitly need a 'this' pointer to the object they're being called on. 

So a member function:
```c++
class MyClass {
public:
  void MyMethod() { }
};
```
Is actually more like this under the hood:
```c++
class MyClass {
public:
  static void MyMethod(MyClass* this) { }
};
```
Templates and functors (or 'function objects') can be of help here.

You can create a Functor class that can be used to execute the method:

```c++
class Functor
{
public:
    virtual void operator()() = 0;  
};
```

After this, you can create Functors to your methods:

```c++
class MyClassMethod : public Functor
{
     MyClass & obj;
public:
     MyClassMethod ( MyClass & obj_) : obj(obj_) {}
     void operator()()  { obj.MyMethod();}  
};
```

Now your Render method can accept Functor:

```c++
void Render(Functor & Call)
{
    D3dDevice->BeginScene();
    Call();
    D3dDevice->EndScene();
    D3dDevice->Present(0,0,0,0);
}
```

And you may use that Render method:

```c++
MyClass obj;
Render(MyClassMethod(obj));
```

However, as of C++11 the simplest way is to use lambdas:

```c++
void Render(std::function<void()> Call)
{
    D3dDevice->BeginScene();
    Call();
    D3dDevice->EndScene();
    D3dDevice->Present(0,0,0,0);
}
```

And then simply pass a lambda that calls your member function as a parameter when you call Render:
```c++
MyClass instance;
Render([&]() { instance.someMethod(); });
```
In this case, `[&]` is a capture clause that means we capture all local variables by reference, `instance` is captured and `someMethod` is called upon it.

Lambdas can be seen as a shorthand for functors and are widely used when it comes to parameters like these.

