Unfortunately, there isn't a direct equivalent in C++ for performing grid search cross validation as used in scikit-learn with Python. Most C++ machine learning libraries like Shark, Dlib and mlpack do not provide this functionality out of the box as Python's scikit-learn does. 

Implementing grid search and cross-validation from scratch in C++ is quite complex and can be time-consuming unless you really need to use C++ for your program. 

That being said, you could technically use a C++ SVM implementation like the one found in Dlib or libSVM and manually implement a loop over the parameters to tune. However, you would also need to manually handle the cross-validation, which is non-trivial.

Alternatively, you might consider using software that allows you to call Python scripts from C++, like Boost.Python or Cython, which would allow you to utilize the power of libraries like Scikit-Learn in a C++ environment.

Here's a rough example of how you would want to iterate over the parameter space and do cross-validation in C++. This is pseudocode and not a complete C++ program; it illustrates the basic process without delving into the complexities of working with a specific C++ SVM library.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <library/svm.hpp> // Assuming "library" is the machine learning library you're using
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Load your data into X and y...
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> X;
    std::vector<int> Y;

    // Choose the paramter sets you want to consider...
    std::vector<int> C_values = {1, 10, 100, 1000};
    std::vector<std::string> kernels = {"linear", "rbf"};

    double best_score = -1;
    int best_C;
    std::string best_kernel;

    // Iterate over the parameter space...
    for (const auto& C : C_values) {
        for (const auto& kernel : kernels) {
            // Create and train your SVM...
            library::SVM classifier;
            classifier.set_C(C);
            classifier.set_kernel(kernel);

            // Perform cross-validation and compute the mean score...
            double score = cross_validate(classifier, X, Y);

            // If this is the best score so far, save the parameters...
            if (score > best_score) {
                best_score = score;
                best_C = C;
