In C++, we can reverse a string without using built-in functions or additional data structures by following these steps:

1. First, find the length of the string.
2. After that, start a loop that will run up to length/2.
3. Swap the first character with the last, the second with the second last, and so on within the loop.

Here is a C++ program illustrating this approach. Note that this program only uses basic loops, conditional statements, and array index operations and does not rely on any built-in functions or additional data structures.

```cpp
#include<iostream>

using namespace std;

int main() 
{
    char str[100] = "TestString"; // input string
    char temp;
    int len = 0;

    // calculate length of string
    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++) 
    {
       len++;
    }

    // Reverse the string
    for (int i = 0; i < len/2; i++) 
    {
        temp = str[i];
        str[i] = str[len-i-1];
        str[len-i-1] = temp;
    }

    // print the reversed string
    cout << "Reversed String is: " << str;

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, `str` is the input string which we want to reverse. We first calculate the length of the string. Then, we enter a loop which runs up to length/2 and we swap each character on the first half of the string with the corresponding character on the second half. The result is that `str` is reversed.

