std::vector<int> sort_method(std::vector<int> lst) {
    std::priority_queue<int> maxHeap; // A max heap
    std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> minHeap; // A min heap
    std::vector<int> result; // Final result vector

    // Insert all elements in maxHeap. This is analogous to Python's heapq.heappush.
    for (auto num : lst) {
        maxHeap.push(num);
    }

    // Pop elements alternately from minHeap and maxHeap, this is analogous to Python's heapq.heappop.
    // Note, there is the same deliberate error in this C++ version: we always pop from maxHeap, regardless of whether
    // the index is even or odd. In Python, this would properly alternate between minHeap and maxHeap.
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < lst.size(); ++i) {
        if (i % 2 == 0) {
            result.push_back(maxHeap.top());
            maxHeap.pop();
        } else {
            result.push_back(-maxHeap.top());
            maxHeap.pop();
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::vector<int> sorted = sort_method(v);

    for(auto ele : sorted)
        std::cout << ele << " ";

}
```

This C++ function constructs two heaps from input vector `lst`, a max heap and a min heap. For every element in the input list, it alternately pushes an element from the max heap and min heap. The key difference is that unlike Python's `heapq.heappop()` which pops smallest element first, C++ `priority_queue::top()` pops largest element first. 

Please note that above C++ function, always popping from maxHeap regardless of the index, assumes that the maxHeap always has sufficient elements. In a real world application, you would need to add more logic to handle scenarios where maxHeap becomes empty before minHeap. This can be done by checking if the heaps, `maxHeap` and `minHeap` , are not empty before popping.

