Here is a simple C++ program that accomplishes the array transpose. It uses the standard template library (STL) vector container to fluently handle rows of different lengths.

In this program, we are making use of the std::vector data structure. It is a sequence container of C++ STL that abstracts arrays.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

// Function to transpose the vectors
vector<vector<int>> transpose(const vector<vector<int>>& input) {
    size_t max_cols = 0;
    for(const auto& row : input) {
        max_cols = max(max_cols, row.size());
    }

    vector<vector<int>> output(max_cols);
    for(size_t col = 0; col < max_cols; ++col) {
        for(const auto& row : input) {
            if(col < row.size()) {
                output[col].push_back(row[col]);
            }
        }
    }

    return output;
}

// Function to print a vector
void print(const vector<vector<int>>& vec) {
    for (const auto& row : vec) {
        for(int val : row) {
            cout << val << ' ';
        }
        cout << '\n';
    }
}

int main() {
    vector<vector<int>> vec = {{1,2,3},{4,5},{6,7,8,9}};
    vector<vector<int>> transposedVec = transpose(vec);

    cout << "Original Vector:\n";
    print(vec);
    cout << "\nTransposed Vector:\n";
    print(transposedVec);
    return 0;
}
```

The function 'transpose' creates a new vector of vectors to house the transposed results, with its size initially set to the maximum column size of the input vector. 

Next, it iterates over each column index within the range of maximum column size and then over each row vector. If the current row vector's size is bigger than the current column index, the value at that index in the row is inserted into the transposed vector at the current column index. 

In the end, the matrix is transposed in that each row of the input vector becomes a column in the output vector.

The function 'print' iterates through each vector and prints each integer followed by a space, ending each row vector with a newline for readability. 

Program 'main' sets up this system by creating an input vector of vectors, calling the transposition function on it, and displaying the results via the print function.

