Here is a simple implementation of a recursive Fibonacci function in C++ using memoization. For this, we make use of an array of size n+1 initialized with the value -1. Then, we use it to save the computed Fibonacci numbers. 

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

vector<int> F; // Global variable to store Fibonacci numbers

int fib(int n) {
    // Base case
    if (n <= 1)
        return n;

    // Check if the fib(n) has already been computed and stored
    if (F[n] != -1)
        return F[n];

    // If not, compute fib(n) and store it in F[n]
    F[n] = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
    return F[n];
}

int main() {
    int n;
    cout << "Enter the position: ";
    cin >> n;

    // Initialize the memoization array
    F.resize(n+1, -1);

    cout << n << "th Fibonacci number is " << fib(n);
    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, the vector F is initialised with -1s. When the function fib() is called with an input n, it first checks if F[n] has a value that is not -1. If F[n] is not -1, it directly returns the stored value F[n], which is the nth Fibonacci number. If F[n] is -1, it computes the nth Fibonacci number by recursively calling fib(n-1) and fib(n-2), and stores the result in F[n], before returning it.
This way, for an input n, fib(n-1) and fib(n-2) don't have to be recalculated for every recursive call, they are simply retrieved from the vector F, thus improving the efficiency of the program.

