    // Insert words into set after converting to lowercase for case insensitivity
    for(std::string word : arr) {
        toLowerCase(word);
        s.insert(word);
    }

    // Transform set into the vector and return
    std::vector<std::string> result(s.begin(), s.end());

    // Convert to original case
    for(int i = 0; i < result.size(); i++) {
        for(int j = 0; j < arr.size(); j++) {
            toLowerCase(arr[j]);
            if(arr[j] == result[i]) {
                result[i] = arr[j];
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> arr = {"", "Stack", "overflow", "", "overflow", "STACK", "Hello"};

    std::vector<std::string> output = countUniqueWords(arr);

    // Display the output
    std::cout << "Unique words are: ";
    for(std::string str : output)
        std::cout << str << " ";
    return 0;
}
```

This function first removes all the empty strings from the given vector. Then, it inserts each word into a set after transforming it into lowercase. The property of a set in C++ is that it only contains unique elements and they are stored in sorted order. Therefore, the resulting set contains distinct words and they are already sorted in alphabetical order. 

We then convert the set back to a vector and return it. Also, the words are recorded in their original case.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n log n), with 'n' being the size of the input array. This is because inserting elements into a set has a time complexity of O(log n) and we're doing this 'n' times. The final conversion from the set to the vector has a time complexity of O(n).

