A unary operator in C++ is an operator that operates on only one operand. Unary operators include ++ (increment), -- (decrement), - (negative), ! (not), and & (address of). These unary operators perform various operations such as incrementing, decrementing, negating, and so on.

Increment and Decrement operators (++ and --)
These operators are used to either increase or decrease the value of the operand by 1. They take the form of either prefix (e.g., ++num) or postfix (e.g., num++).

```
int a = 10;
++a;   //a is now 11
a--;   //a is now 10 again
```

Negative operator (-) 
This operator is used to change the sign of the operand to which it is applied.

``` 
int b = 10;
b = -b; //b is now -10
```

Not operator (!)
This operator returns true if the operand is 0 (false) and false if it is anything else (true).

```
bool c = false;
!c;   // Returns true
```

Address-of operator (&)
Returns the memory address of its operand.

```
int d = 10;
cout << &d;  // Prints the memory address of variable d
```

Now, let's write a program that uses these unary operators. For example, a simple program that decrypts a given encrypted string would be a good example to illustrate their utility. The decryption scheme is simple: we decrease the ASCII value of characters with even indexes by 1 and increase those with odd indexes by 1. 

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

std::string decrypt(std::string encrypted) {
    for (int i = 0; i < encrypted.length(); ++i) {
        if ((i & 1) == 0) // here '&' is used as a binary operator ('and') not a unary operator
            --encrypted[i];
        else
            ++encrypted[i];
    }
    return encrypted;
}

int main() {
    std::string encrypted = "abpzjrezzfg";
    std::string decrypted = decrypt(encrypted);
    std::cout << "Decrypted string: " << decrypted << std::endl; // prints "a boy is free" 
    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we use several unary operators: we used increment and decrement operators to adjust ASCII values and the address-of operator (&) to get the address of the data, but it is used as a binary operator.

Exceptions and Limitations:
While unary operators are powerful, they can be misused easily. For example, using increment/decrement operators (++ and --) on a constant operand will lead to a compilation error. Similarly, without proper type checking, you may end up applying unary operators (like negation operator) on non-numeric types which might lead to run-time errors. It is also important to remember the sequence of operation in case of post increment/decrement and pre increment/decrement.
Another key thing to note is the side effect of the post-increment and post-decrement operators: they will return the value before applying the increment/decrement operation. This can lead to potentially hard-to-spot bugs in your code if not used carefully.

