Here is a simple C++ code that implements a rotation shift algorithm on an array in a circular manner:

```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Function to rotate array to the left
void rotateLeft(int arr[], int n, int k)
{
    /* To get the starting point of rotated array */
    int mod = k % n;

    // Prints the rotated array from starting position
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        cout << (arr[(mod + i) % n]) << " ";

    cout << "\n";
}

// Driver Code
int main() 
{ 
    int arr[] = { 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 }; 
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]); 
    int k = 2; 
    rotateLeft(arr, n, k); 
    return 0; 
} 
```

In this code, we shift the array elements towards left by `k` places. The important point here is "shifting is done in a circular manner". That's why we use the modulus operator (`%`), which makes it a circular rotation.

The `rotateLeft` function takes an array `arr[ ]`, the number of elements in the array `n` and the number of positions `k` by which the array should be shifted. It then starts printing elements of the rotated array from the starting position (obtained by `k` % `n`) by incrementing it circularly (`(mod + i) % n`) for `n` times.

To rotate the array towards the right, you just need to change `(mod + i)` to `(n - mod + i)`. Other than that, the logic remains the same.

