In STL, removing an element by value is usually done using a combination of the `std::remove` algorithm and the `erase` method of the vector. 

The reason why there's no built-in method in the vector class to remove an element by value is due to STL's philosophy of separating data structure and algorithms. In other words, the idea is to provide generic algorithms that can work with various container types, not just vectors.

Here's how you'd typically do it:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>

std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 2, 5};
int value_to_remove = 2;

v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), value_to_remove), v.end());
```

In the above line, `std::remove` moves all elements that are not equal to `value_to_remove` to the beginning of the vector and returns an iterator pointing to the new end of the vector. Then, `v.erase` removes all elements from this new end to the actual end of the vector.

Note that `std::remove` doesn't actually remove any elements from the vector -- it just rearranges the elements so that the "non-removed" elements come before the "removed" ones. The `erase` method is what actually removes the elements from the vector.

