C++ version:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

std::pair<int, int> find_max(std::vector<int>& numbers) {
    std::map<int, std::pair<int, int>, std::greater<int>> maxMap;
    // loop to calculate the count of unique digits for each number and store in a map.
    for(int i = 0; i < numbers.size(); i++){
        std::string num_str = std::to_string(numbers[i]);
        std::sort(num_str.begin(), num_str.end());
        num_str.erase(std::unique(num_str.begin(), num_str.end()), num_str.end());

        int unique_count = num_str.length();
        // Using negative count as key to sort in descending order. In case of tie, earliest number is returned as map keeps the inserted sequence in case of collision.
        maxMap[-unique_count] = {numbers[i], unique_count};
    }

    return maxMap.begin()->second;
}
```

In the code above, a `map` is used to keep track of the unique count and its corresponding number. `map` in C++ by default sorts its elements by the key in ascending order. But here we need the maximum number with maximum unique digits count, hence the key used is negation of the count of unique digits. This gives the map in descending order of count of unique digits. If there are multiple numbers with the same count of unique digits, the one with smallest index (earliest) in the vector is stored as map insert operation doesn't overwrite the value for a pre-existing key. Hence in case of tie, we get the number with smallest index.

I hope this is helpful. Do note that map operations can be slow, and if you're dealing with large dataset, there may be more efficient solutions.

