A namespace is a declarative region in C++ that provides a scope to the identifiers (names of types, functions, variables etc.) inside it. Namespaces are used to organize code into logical groups and to prevent name collisions that can occur especially when your code base includes multiple libraries.

Following are the main purposes and functionalities of a namespace in C++:

1. Scopes: A namespace is a region of code which can hold its own variables, objects and functions. These items can have the same names as those in other namespaces without causing a name collision.

2. Preventing name conflicts: Namespaces are mainly used for avoiding collision among variable names. Without a namespace, a global variable declared in one part of a program could conflict with a similarly named variable in another part of the program. Having different namespaces resolves this issue.

3. Code organization: Namespaces are also used to organize code into logical, easy-to-understand blocks. Each namespace can contain a related group of variables, functions and classes.

An example of a namespace usage is the 'std' namespace in C++. 

The 'std' namespace includes features of the C++ Standard Library - such as cout, cin and string. When you use these features, you typically specify that they're in the 'std' namespace by using the scope operator "::" (for example, std::cout) or by declaring "using namespace std;" at the start of your program (which tells C++ to assume that you're using the 'std' namespace for the rest of your program).

```c++
