In C++, there's a way to swap values between two character variables in C++ without using a separate temporary variable or arithmetic operators by using XOR bitwise operation. Please note that XOR operation is still an operation, but it is not an arithmetic operation (+, -, *, /, etc.), but a bitwise one, which means it operates on the binary representation of data.

Given two variables, swapping their values using XOR operations is accomplished as follows:

```cpp
char1 = char1 ^ char2;   // bitwise XOR, new value of char1 is now char1 XOR char2
char2 = char1 ^ char2;   // because char1 is now char1 XOR char2, char2 becomes char1
char1 = char1 ^ char2;   // char1 becomes char2
```

An implementation of the above operations in C++ code:
```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    char char1, char2;

    cout << "Enter two characters: ";
    cin >> char1 >> char2;

    if (cin.fail()) {
        cout << "Error: Invalid input. Must be two characters." << endl;
        return 1;  
    }

    char1 = char1 ^ char2;
    char2 = char1 ^ char2;
    char1 = char1 ^ char2;

    cout << "After swapping: " << char1 << " " << char2 << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Regarding error handling:
In the provided code, we're using cin.fail() function which is built-in into iostream standard library in C++. This function returns true when the previous cin input operation failed due to invalid input type (in this case anything other than characters for char1 and char2 could cause an error). When this happens, we're printing an error message and returning an error code (1) to indicate that our program didn't succeed.

Please note that using XOR to swap values is very specific and not often used. This is because it violates some of the good programming practices, particularly regarding readability, explicitness and handling edge cases (if both variables refer to the same memory location the result would become zero).

