Below are two functions: `reverse` and `merge`. The `reverse` function simply swaps the next and prev pointers for all nodes in the doubly linked list. The `merge` function is a bit more complex. It works similar to the merge process of merge-sort, where two sorted arrays are merged to create one sorted array.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;

    Node(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = prev = NULL;
    }
};

Node* reverse(Node* head) {
    Node* temp = NULL;  
    Node* current = head;

    // swap next and prev for all nodes
    while (current != NULL) {
        temp = current->prev;
        current->prev = current->next;
        current->next = temp;
        current = current->prev;
    }

    // fix head at the end
    if(temp != NULL )
        head = temp->prev;

    return head;
}

Node* merge(Node* first, Node* second) {
    // if first linked list is empty
    if (!first)
        return second;

    // if second linked list is empty
    if (!second)
        return first;

    // pick the smaller value
    if (first->data < second->data) {
        first->next = merge(first->next, second);
        first->next->prev = first;
        first->prev = NULL;
        return first;
    } else {
        second->next = merge(first, second->next);
        second->next->prev = second;
        second->prev = NULL;
        return second;
    }
}
```

Each function handles null values and makes sure that all next and prev pointers are updated accordingly.

The `merge` function merges two sorted linked lists, so if the inputs are not sorted, the result would also be unsorted. 

Finally, the time complexity of `reverse` function is O(n) where n is the number of nodes in the list and the time complexity of `merge` function is also O(n) where n is the total number of nodes in the two lists. It's important to note that merging function assumes that input lists are already sorted.

