The Collatz conjecture procedure is quite simple, for a given number n, if n is even, the next number in the sequence is n/2 and if n is odd, the next number is 3n+1. The conjecture goes that no matter the starting number, the sequence will always reach 1.

Here is a simple C++ code that implements the Collatz conjecture:

```C++
#include <iostream>

void collatz_sequence(int n) {
    // Check if n is a positive number
    if (n <= 0) {
        std::cout << "Number should be positive.";
        return;
    }

    while (n != 1) {
        std::cout << n << " ";

        // If number is even
        if (n % 2 == 0)
            n = n / 2;

        // If number is odd 
        else
            n = 3 * n + 1;
    }

    std::cout << n;
}

int main() {
    int number;
    std::cout << "Enter a positive number: ";
    std::cin >> number;

    std::cout << "Collatz conjecture sequence: ";
    collatz_sequence(number);

    return 0;
}
