Nonetheless, switch-case statements also come with limitations. Handling cases that involve non-integers, i.e., strings or floats, is nontrivial. If decisions are based around options that aren’t so well defined or easy to number, if-else conditionals may be required to handle these cases.

When there are several cases, a switch case can be cleaner than encoding the same logic with if-else statements. However, if-else statements are more flexible as they can handle ranges and more complex conditions.

The choice between a switch-case and an if-else construct should depend on the specific requirements of the problem you are trying to solve. In games with more complex decision-making or character AI, control flow designs such as state machines could be much more efficient and suited than nested switch-case or if-else constructs.

