#include <immintrin.h>

constexpr bool simd_char_match(char c, const char* char_set, size_t set_size) {
    __m128i input = _mm_set1_epi8(c);  // Broadcast input character to all lanes of the SIMD register
    size_t i = 0;
    for (; i + 16 <= set_size; i += 16) {
        __m128i chunk = _mm_loadu_si128(reinterpret_cast<const __m128i*>(char_set + i));  // Load 16 characters from char_set
        __m128i result = _mm_cmpeq_epi8(input, chunk);  // Compare input with the chunk
        int mask = _mm_movemask_epi8(result);  // Generate a mask of matching positions
        if (mask != 0) {
            return true;  // If any match is found, return true
        }
    }
    for (; i < set_size; ++i) {
        if (char_set[i] == c) {
            return true;  // Linear search for any remaining characters
        }
    }
    return false;  // No match found
}
```

In the solution, we use the SSE2 SIMD instructions to efficiently compare the input character with the characters in the `char_set`. The function processes the characters in `char_set` in chunks of 16 characters at a time, using SIMD parallelism to compare them with the input character. If any match is found, the function returns true. If no match is found, it falls back to a linear search for any remaining characters in the set. This approach leverages SIMD parallelism to efficiently perform character set matching.

Here is a basic example of a C++ program that combines Fibonacci sequence, prime checking and memoization techniques.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> primes;

bool isPrime(int n) {
    if (primes.empty())
        primes.push_back(2);

    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < primes.size(); i++) {
        if ((n != primes[i]) && (n % primes[i] == 0))
            return false;
        if (primes[i] * primes[i] > n) {
            primes.push_back(n);
            return true;
        }
    }

    primes.push_back(n);
    return true;
}

std::vector<int> memo;

int fibonacci(int n) {
    if (memo[n] != -1)
        return memo[n];

    if(n <= 1)
        return n;
    else
        memo[n] = fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);

    return memo[n];
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter number of elements to display: ";
    std::cin >> n;

    memo.resize(n + 1, -1);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int fibNum = fibonacci(i);
        if (isPrime(fibNum))
            std::cout << fibNum << " ";
    }

    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This program asks the user to input a number and generates that many Fibonacci numbers and checks if each number is prime. If it is prime, it prints it. 

The program uses two vectors - `primes` and `memo`. 

- `isPrime` function utilizes the `primes` vector to store previously discovered prime numbers and checks the primality of a given number by checking it only against previously stored prime numbers. It greatly improves the efficiency of the prime checking function. 

- `fibonacci` function utilizes the `memo` vector for memoization. Memoization is an optimization technique which speeds up the program by storing the results of expensive function calls. It avoids the repeated calculations of the same inputs by returning the cached value when the same inputs occur again. 

The `memo` vector is initialized with `-1` for all indices. When a fibonacci number is calculated, it is stored in the corresponding index. If a fibonacci number is already calculated, it is directly returned from the `memo` vector without doing the same calculations again.

Please note that the Fibonacci Sequence starts from 0 and 1, both numbers are not considered prime numbers. If you want to consider only prime fibonacci numbers, the output will be empty for the small input sizes and take a while to compute visible results because prime fibonacci numbers are rare.

