In this problem, two separate tasks are defined: (1) eliminating an element with a specific value from a bidirectional linked list and (2) reorganizing the linked list around a pivot value while maintaining the original relative order of elements. 

Here is a C++ solution that addresses the aforementioned tasks.

Consider that Node is a structure for the linked list with an integer data value and pointers to the next and previous nodes.

```cpp
#include<iostream>

// Linked List Node
struct Node
{
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;	
};

// Function to eliminate element with specific value
void deleteNode(Node** head_ref, Node* del)
{
    if (*head_ref == NULL || del == NULL)
        return;

    if (*head_ref == del)
        *head_ref = del->next;

    if (del->next != NULL)
        del->next->prev = del->prev;

    if (del->prev != NULL)
        del->prev->next = del->next;     

    free(del);
    return;
}

// Function to reorganize linked list around pivot
Node* partition(Node* head, Node* tail, int pivot)
{
    Node* prevNode = new Node();
    prevNode->next = head;

    Node* curr = head;
    Node* end = tail->next;

    while(curr != end)
    {
        if(curr->data < pivot)
        {
            prevNode = curr;
            curr = curr->next;
        }
        else
        {
            if(curr == end)
                end = curr->prev;
            deleteNode(&head, curr);

            Node* nextNode = curr->next;
            curr->next = end->next;

            if(end->next != NULL)
                end->next->prev = curr;

            end->next = curr;
            curr->prev = end;
            curr = nextNode;
        }
    }
    return (prevNode->data >= pivot)? prevNode: prevNode->next;
}

// Function to print linked list
void printList(Node* node)
{
    while(node != NULL)
    {
        std::cout << node->data << " ";
        node = node->next;
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
}
```
You will use `deleteNode()` function to eliminate a node from the list, `partition()` to rearrange the list around the pivot and `printList()` to print the list at any given state.
Remember that you need to create the utility functions to insert a node at the end of the list and to handle the list itself.
This solution presents a complexity of O(N), where N is the number of nodes, due to the linear scanning of the linked list to rearrange it around the pivot.

