Here is an optimized in place string reversal implementation in C++. The complexity for this solution is O(n) where n is the length of the string. It uses two pointers moving towards the center of the string and ensures only alphabetic characters are reversed by verifying if the characters are valid alphabetic characters using the built-in isalpha() function:

```c++
#include <cctype> // for isalpha

void reverseStringExcludingSpecialChars(char str[])
{
    int r = strlen(str) - 1, l = 0;

    while (l < r)
    {
        if (!isalpha(str[l]))  // check if left pointer character is not an alphabetic character
            l++;
        else if (!isalpha(str[r]))  // check if right pointer character is not an alphabetic character
            r--;
        else  // if both are alphabetic characters, swap them
        {
            std::swap(str[l], str[r]);
            l++;
            r--;
        }
    }
}
```
This function will accept a null-terminated string as input and modify it in place to have its alphabetic characters reversed, non-alphabetic characters will remain in the same place. Note that this function works on the assumption that the input string is ASCII; it might not work as expected with Unicode or other multi-byte strings.

