This is your solution in C++ using the threading and mutex libraries. It's important to note that the order of execution of threads in multithreading is decided by the scheduler and we have no control over it. But we can guarantee the order of execution using various synchronization techniques like mutex and condition variables.

```C++
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable cv;
int index = 0;
std::string s = "Hello World";

void printChar(int i) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
    cv.wait(lock, [i] { return i == index; });
    std::cout << s[i];
    ++index;
    lock.unlock();
    cv.notify_all();
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for(int i = 0; i < s.length(); ++i) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(printChar, i));
    }

    for(auto& t : threads) {
        t.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Explanation
- A mutex `m` is declared to allow mutual exclusion.
- A condition variable `cv` is declared to put threads into sleep and wake them up.
- A global index `index` is declared to control the order of thread execution.
- The string `s = "Hello World"` is passed to the threads.
- The `printChar` function waits for its turn i.e., until its index is equal to global `index`.
- In `main`, for each character of the `s`, a new thread is created that runs `printChar`.
- The `join` function is used to ensure that the main function waits until all threads are finished.

