Create a C++ code that performs a circular shift on an integer array of specific elements by a designated number of positions.
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int k = 2;

a:
Here is a simple C++ code that performs a circular shift on an integer array of specific elements by a designated number of positions:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void shiftArray(int arr[], int n, int k) {
    int temp[k];

    for(int i=0; i<k; i++) {  
        temp[i] = arr[i];
    }

    for(int i=0; i<n-k; i++) { 
        arr[i] = arr[i+k];
    }

    for(int i=n-k, j=0; i<n; i++, j++) {  
        arr[i] = temp[j];
    }
}

void displayArray(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int k = 2;
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "Array before shifting: ";
    displayArray(arr, n);

    shiftArray(arr, n, k);

    cout << "Array after shifting: ";
    displayArray(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, firstly we create a temporary array 'temp' and store the first 'k' elements of the array in it. Then we move the remaining elements in the array to their new positions. And finally we append the 'k' elements from the 'temp' array to the remaining positions in the original array. Hence our original array 'arr' is circularly shifted 'k' positions.

Also, please note that the above code shifts the array to the left by 'k' positions. If you need to shift to the right, you would need to adjust the code accordingly.

