The following C++ code provides a binary search algorithm for a sorted string array:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

int binarySearch(std::vector<std::string>& arr, std::string target)
{
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.size() - 1;

    while (left <= right)
    {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target)
        {
            return mid;
        }
        else if (arr[mid] < target)
        {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
        else
        {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    // Given string is not found
    return -1;
}

int main()
{
    std::vector<std::string> arr = {"apple", "banana", "cherry", "date", "elderberry"};
    std::string target = "date";

    int result = binarySearch(arr, target);

    if(result != -1)
        std::cout << "Element found at index: " << result;
    else
        std::cout << "Element not found in array";

    return 0;
}
```
Note: The array needs to be sorted for binary search to work. If the input array is not sorted, we will need to sort it which will add additional time overhead.

Time Complexity: Binary Search has a time complexity of O(log n) in all scenarios (best, average, and worst case). This is achieved by halving the search space at every step.

Space Complexity: This implementation of the Binary Search algorithm is Iterative and doesn't use any additional space to solve the problem. Therefore, it achieves the space complexity of O(1).
Remember, this code assumes that the input string array is sorted. If it isn't sorted, you need to sort it before using a binary search which will affect time complexity.

