Namespaces in C++, like packages in Java, are used to group together related classes, functions, and other identifiers, to avoid naming clashing in large projects.

It is a common practice in C++ to create namespaces for major components of your project. Ideally, each logical block or module of your project would have its own namespace.

Here's an example of how namespaces can be defined and used in a C++ program:

```cpp
// a C++ namespace definition
namespace MyNamespace {
  class MyClass {
  public:
    void myFunction();
  };
}

// using class from the namespace
int main() {
  MyNamespace::MyClass myObject;
  myObject.myFunction();
  return 0;
}
```

You can use the `using` directive to avoid having to use the `namespace::` prefix.

```cpp
using namespace MyNamespace;
```

This line in the code will make all the identifiers from the `MyNamespace` available so you can use them directly without providing a `namespace::` prefix. However, be careful, because if there are multiple namespaces used with the `using` directive, and any of them have overlapping names, it may cause a clash.

You can also use the `using` directive to bring individual identifiers into scope:

```cpp
using MyNamespace::MyClass;
```

This will bring `MyClass` into the scope, and you can use `MyClass` directly without `namespace::` prefix.

Regarding the question whether to create a namespace for the whole application or for major components, both are common practices and it mainly depends on your project design, size, and the discretion of your team.

Usually, small projects can work well with a single namespace for the entire application. However, if your project is substantial, it is more efficient and clearer to use namespaces for individual major components to avoid naming clashes and maintain clear structure. Namespaces can be nested as well, if necessary.

