```cpp

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>

// shared variables
std::condition_variable cv;
std::mutex mtx;
std::string message = "Hello, World!";
int globalIndex = 0;

void print_char(int charIndex){
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lck(mtx);

    // The predicate is checked at each wait, making the operation thread-safe
    cv.wait(lck, [charIndex](){ return globalIndex == charIndex; }); // wait until it's this thread's turn

    std::cout << message[charIndex];

    globalIndex++; // increment shared index
    cv.notify_all(); // notify other threads it's potentially their turn
}

int main(){
   // Create threads for each one char from string "Hello, World!"
   std::thread Threads[message.size()];
   for (size_t i = 0; i < message.size(); ++i) {
     Threads[i] = std::thread(print_char, i);
   }

   // Join the threads
   for (auto& th : Threads) {
     th.join();
   }

   std::cout << "\n";

   return 0;
}
```

The concept here is to set up a condition variable `cv` and use it with a mutex `mtx` to prevent race conditions. We create each thread to print its corresponding character once it has been signaled by the previous thread that incremented the shared `globalIndex`.

Each thread waits its turn based on its passed `charIndex` value and the shared `globalIndex`. The thread corresponding to the current `globalIndex` proceeds to print its character, increment `globalIndex`, and then notify other threads that the `globalIndex has been incremented. All threads waiting for their turn will then re-check the condition and one will proceed.

This results in the characters printed in the correct order for the message "Hello, World!".

