Implementing a function to evaluate mathematical expressions from string in C++ is quite involved and tough task when we don't use any in-built or third-party library functions for parsing or evaluating mathematical expressions. It needs to implement parsing technique by defining grammar for our mathematical expression. A simplified version of the problem can be solved if we only allow integer inputs for simplicity.

Below is example of a function that would parse an expression string and perform mathematical operations as per precedence & associativity rules (BIDMAS). Furthermore, it implements error handling for invalid mathematical expressions and division by zero. Please note that it is simplified version and it doesn't deal with power operator, but this framework can be extended to support it.

Note that for simplicity purposes this function will not contain full error checking or be able to handle any kind of input. For example, the input should contain no spaces, only contain numbers, parentheses and the four main binary operators. This function does not deal with unary operators.

```cpp
#include <exception>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stack>

int precedence(char op){
   if(op == '^')
      return 3;
   else if(op == '*' || op == '/')
      return 2;
   else if(op == '+' || op == '-')
      return 1;
   return 0;
}

double applyOperation(double a, double b, char op){
   switch(op){
      case '+':
         return a + b;
      case '-':
         return a - b;
      case '*':
         return a * b;
      case '/':
         if(b == 0)
            throw std::invalid_argument("Division by zero is not allowed");
         return a / b;
   }
   return 0;
}

double evaluate(std::string exp){
   std::stack<double> operands;
   std::stack<char> operators;

   for(int i=0; i<exp.length(); i++){
      if(exp[i] == '('){
         operators.push(exp[i]);
      }
      else if(isdigit(exp[i])){
         double value = 0;
         while(i<exp.length() && isdigit(exp[i])){
            value = (value*10) + (exp[i]-'0');
            i++;
         }
         i--;
         operands.push(value);
      }
      else if(exp[i] == ')'){
         while(!operators.empty() && operators.top()!='('){
            double operand2 = operands.top();
            operands.pop();

            double operand1 = operands.top(); 
            operands.pop();

            char op = operators.top(); 
            operators.pop();

            operands.push(applyOperation(operand1, operand2, op));
         }
         if(!operators.empty())
           operators.pop();
      }
      else{
         while(!operators.empty() && precedence(operators.top()) >= precedence(exp[i])){
            double operand2 = operands.top();
            operands.pop();

            double operand1 = operands.top();
            operands.pop();

            char op = operators.top();
            operators.pop();

            operands.push(applyOperation(operand1, operand2, op));
         }
         operators.push(exp[i]);
      }
   }
   while(!operators.empty()){
      double operand2 = operands.top();
      operands.pop();

      double operand1 = operands.top();
      operands.pop();

      char op = operators.top();
      operators.pop();

      operands.push(applyOperation(operand1, operand2, op));
   }
   return operands.top();
}

int main(){
   std::string exp;
   std::cin>>exp;

   try{
      std::cout<<evaluate(exp);
   }catch(const std::exception &e){
      std::cerr << "\nCaught exception: " << e.what() << std::endl;
   }

   return 0;
}
```
This above code uses two stacks, one for operations and one for operands. For each character in the string it pushes numbers onto the operand stack and operations onto the operation stack. When the code sees a closing parenthesis or an operation with lower priority it begins calculating the current subexpression and replaces it with the result by popping values from both stacks. It then continues processing the string until it has finished. When the string has finished the result of the expression will be the top of the operands stack.

