Here is the equivalent C++ code to achieve the same functionality as your Python snippet.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <set>
using namespace std;

void quick_sort(vector<int>& v, int start, int end) {
    if (start < end) {
        int pivot = v[end];
        int i = (start - 1);

        for(int j = start; j <= end - 1; j++) {
            if (v[j] < pivot) {
                i++;
                swap(v[i], v[j]);
            }
        }
        swap(v[i + 1], v[end]);
        int pi = (i + 1);

        quick_sort(v, start, pi - 1);
        quick_sort(v, pi + 1, end);
    }
}

vector<int> merged_list(vector<int>& list1, vector<int>& list2) {
    vector<int> nums;
    nums.reserve(list1.size() + list2.size());
    nums.insert(nums.end(), list1.begin(), list1.end());
    nums.insert(nums.end(), list2.begin(), list2.end());

    quick_sort(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1);

    set<int> s(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    nums.assign(s.begin(), s.end());

    reverse(nums.begin(), nums.end());

    return nums;
}
```

Notes:
1. In C++, we can handle lists with C++ STL (Standard Template Library) vectors.
2. We used STL algorithm library to sort, remove duplicates, and reverse the list, rather than manually coding these.
3. We used std::set to eliminate duplicate numbers from the list (std::set only stores unique elements).
4. std::swap is used to swap items in quick sort function.
5. The quick_sort() function is modified in such a way that it takes vector along with the start and end position as input. The original Python code does this differently. Python's list splicing makes it easy to sub-divide the list directly in the recursive call and it uses list comprehension which isn't convenient in C++. Instead, in C++ version, start and end parameters for array (list in Python) are explicitly used.

