Instead of using 'const', C# uses 'readonly' to indicate that a class field can only be set during construction and can't be changed afterwards. Additionally, arguments passed to a method in C# are 'const' by default in the sense that they can't be reassigned within the method.

For instance, in C++, you might mark a method as const to indicate that it will not modify the state of the object:

```c++
