{
    void DoSomething(); // Don't modify the state of the object
};
```

So the responsibility to ensure 'const correctness' in C# mainly falls on good design, careful programming, and code reviews.

For complex data structures, consider using immutable data structures which cannot be changed once they are created. This provides a similar guarantee as 'const' in that any modifications will result a copy being created with the desired changes, leaving the original structure untouched.

