Given the complexity of the problem, a full code solution is difficult to provide in a discussion format, but I will lay out a way to approach the problem.

The STL vector is a powerful class that we can use to manage dynamic multi-dimensional arrays. This is because vectors are resizable arrays and can have other vectors as elements, thus can represent multi-dimensional arrays. We can write a class `MultiDimArray`, which represents a multi-dimensional array and utilizes nested vectors to achieve this.

Below is a simplified blueprint of the class that demonstrates the main concept:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

class MultiDimArray {
private:
    std::vector<void*> array; // main array
    std::vector<int> lengths; // lengths of dimensions
    int dimensions; // number of dimensions

public:
    // constructor
    MultiDimArray(std::vector<int> sizes) {
        dimensions = sizes.size();
        lengths = sizes;
        array.resize(sizes[0]);
        for (int i=0; i<sizes[0]; i++) {
            createArray(&array[i], 1, sizes);
        }
    }

    // destructor
    ~MultiDimArray() {
        deleteArray(&array, lengths);
    }

    // recursive function to create the array
    void createArray(void** arr, int dim, std::vector<int> sizes) {
        if (dim == dimensions-1) {
            *arr = new int[sizes[dim]]; 
        } else {
            *arr = new void*[sizes[dim]];
            for (int i=0; i<sizes[dim]; i++) {
                createArray((void**)(*arr)+i, dim+1, sizes);
            }
        }
    }

    // recursive function to delete the array
    void deleteArray(void** arr, std::vector<int> lengths) {
        if (lengths.size() > 1) {
            std::vector<int> sub_lengths(lengths.begin()+1, lengths.end());
            for (int i=0; i<lengths[0]; i++) {
                deleteArray((void**)(*arr)+i, sub_lengths);
            }
        }
        delete [] *arr;
    }
};
```

This code is a basic as it just deals with array allocation and deallocation. More functions for array element access, assignment, traversing etc. would be needed in a complete implementation.

Please be aware, this code is mainly illustrative in nature and is a bit simplified. It doesn't handle error checking and is somewhat 'type-unsafe'. I would not recommend using `void*` casts in production code as these are dangerous and often lead to memory leaks or undefined behavior if not used carefully. Plus it doesn't conform to modern C++ idioms and best practices.

For real-world usage, A library like boost::multi_array reference, https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_47_0/libs/multi_array/doc/ would be a far better choice. If boost is not an option then a template class which constructs an N dimensional array out of std::vector should be considered.

