Here is a simple program using two threads to print odd and even numbers in sequence. This program will use std::condition_variable for synchronization between the two threads.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>

std::condition_variable cond;
std::mutex mutex;
bool printOdd = true; // flag to check what to print next

void printEvenNumbers() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
    for (int i = 2; i <= 100; i += 2) {
        // Sleep this thread until printOdd is false
        cond.wait(lock, [] { return !printOdd; });
        std::cout << "Even number: " << i << std::endl;
        printOdd = true;
        // Notify other thread (odd number printing thread)
        cond.notify_all();
    }
}

void printOddNumbers() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
    for (int i = 1; i < 100; i += 2) {
        // Sleep this thread until printOdd is true
        cond.wait(lock, [] { return printOdd; });
        std::cout << "Odd number: " << i << std::endl;
        printOdd = false;
        // Notify other thread (even number printing thread)
        cond.notify_all();
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(printOddNumbers);
    std::thread t2(printEvenNumbers);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, two threads (t1 and t2) are created. t1 calls the function printOddNumbers() and t2 calls the function printEvenNumbers().

std::condition_variable is used for inter-thread synchronization. printOdd is a global flag that helps in deciding which thread should execute next. If printOdd is true, then the odd number printing thread will execute, else the even number printing thread will execute. std::condition_variable's wait function is used to put the currently running thread to sleep and release the mutex until the condition in the lambda function in wait() becomes true. The notify_all() function is used to awaken the sleeping thread.

This synchronization mechanism ensures that both threads will print numbers alternatively (odd-even or even-odd) and it guarantees that the sequence is correct because only one thread can change the shared data at a time due to the mutex lock.

