A pangram is a sentence or expression that uses every letter of the alphabet at least once. The classic example in English is: "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog." 

Here's how you can write a C++ function to determine if a given string is a pangram:

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h> 
using namespace std; 

// A function to check if a string str is a pangram
bool isPangram(char str[], int n) {
    // Create a hash table to mark the presence of each character
    vector<bool> mark(26, false);

    // Traverse all characters
    for (int i=0; i<n; i++) {
        // If it's a lowercase letter, subtract a.
        if ('a' <= str[i] && str[i] <= 'z') 
            mark[str[i] - 'a'] = true;
        // If it's an uppercase letter, subtract A.
        if ('A' <= str[i] && str[i] <= 'Z')
            mark[str[i] - 'A'] = true;
    }

    // Return false if any character is unmarked
    for (int i=0; i<=25; i++) 
        if (mark[i] == false) 
            return (false);

    // If all characters were present
    return (true);
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
    int n = sizeof(str)/sizeof(str[0]);
    if (isPangram(str, n-1)) 
        cout << "It is a pangram." << endl;
    else
        cout << "It is not a pangram." << endl;

    return(0);
}
```

This function creates a boolean array that marks the presence of each character in the string. We iterate through the string and mark each character we find. If all characters are marked at the end, the string is a pangram.

This code takes into account the possibility of mixed case letters and ignores characters that are not English alphabets. 

Keep in mind that this code is case insensitive. If you want to make it case sensitive (i.e., each letter must appear at least once in both its lowercase and uppercase form), you would need to create separate `mark` arrays for lowercase and uppercase letters.

As a standard practice in modern C++ programming, STL library is used for its vector container, and also to include all standard libraries in one go by using `#include <bits/stdc++.h>`.

