```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

bool isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1)
        return false;

    for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
        if (num % i == 0)
            return false;
    }

    return true;
}

int sumPrime(int N) {
    int sum = 0;
    int count = 0;
    int num = 2;

    while(count < N) {
        if (isPrime(num)) {
            sum += num;
            count++;
        }

        num++;
    }

    return sum;
}

int main() {
    int N;

    cout << "Enter the value of N: ";
    cin >> N;

    cout << "The sum of first " << N << " prime numbers is: " << sumPrime(N) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
In this program, we use a helper function `isPrime()` to check if a given number is prime. In the `sumPrime()` function, we use a while loop to keep adding prime numbers to our sum until we have added `N` prime numbers. 

Please note that this solution may not be efficient for large value of `N` because we are checking every number individually for being prime which is computationally expensive. A more efficient approach would be to use a sieve algorithm to generate primes and then add them up.

