    return static_cast<double>(rand()) / (RAND_MAX / 10);
}
```

How it works?
This program begins by seeding the random-number generator with the current time. This is done by calling srand() with an argument that changes every time the program is run.

Then, the program prints out ten random numbers between 0 and 10. To get these random number, we use the getRandomNumber() function. This function call rand() to get a random number, then divide the result by (RAND_MAX / 10) (the maximum value rand() can return) to get a floating-point number between 0 and 10. 

We use static_cast to cast the integer number rand() returns to a double so that we can get a floating-point number between 0 and 10, not just an integer. 

The result is that each time this program is run, you should see ten different numbers output each time (because the program is seeded differently each time). 

Please note that using rand() and srand() is generally not recommended for generating random numbers, due to the quality of randomness and it's not guaranteed to be uniform. Nowadays, modern C++ style recommends to use the <random> library instead. However, for simple uses, rand() and srand() may still be adequate.

