```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>

using namespace std;

pair<vector<int>, vector<int>> sort_vector(vector<int> arr) {
    auto absoluteDigitSum = [] (int num) {
        num = abs(num);
        int ans = 0;
        while(num > 0) {
            ans += num % 10;
            num /= 10;
        }
        return ans;
    };

    auto compare = [&] (int a, int b) {
        int valA = absoluteDigitSum(a);
        int valB = absoluteDigitSum(b);
        return valA < valB || (valA == valB && a < b);
    };

    // Sorting in ascending order
    sort(arr.begin(), arr.end(), compare);

    vector<int> asc = arr;

    // Sorting in descending order
    reverse(arr.begin(), arr.end());

    vector<int> desc = arr;

    return {asc, desc};
}
```
Firstly, we defined a lambda function absoluteDigitSum that returns the sum of absolute values of digits of an integer. Then, another lambda function compare is defined that uses the previous function. It compares two numbers by firstly comparing their absolute digit sums and then comparing their values if the sums are equal.

Then, we sort the array in ascending order using the compare function. After that, we reverse the sorted array to get the array sorted in descending order.

Finally, we return the sorted arrays in ascending and descending orders.

Time complexity is O(n*log(n)) due to sorting where n is the size of the input array.

