In order to handle complex roots, we'll need to #include<complex> at the beginning of our code. Also, since C++ doesn't have a builtin polynomial solving algorithm, we'll need to use an iterative method like the Durand-Kerner method. The Durand-Kerner method is simple enough. We start with an initial guess for our complex root and update it with the equation "root = root - poly(root) / derivative(poly, root)" until we've gotten close enough to the actual root. 

```cpp
#include<iostream>
#include<vector>
#include<complex>
#include<math.h>
using namespace std;

typedef complex<double> cd;

cd poly(vector<cd>& xs, cd x) 
{
    cd result = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < xs.size(); i++)
        result += xs[i] * pow(x, i);

    return result;
}

cd poly_derivative(vector<cd>& xs, cd x) 
{
    cd result = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i < xs.size(); i++) 
        result += xs[i] * i * pow(x, i - 1);

    return result;
}

cd find_zero(vector<cd>& xs, cd initial_guess = 1)
{
    cd root = initial_guess;
    while (abs(poly(xs, root)) > 1e-6)
        root = root - poly(xs, root) / poly_derivative(xs, root);

    return root;
}

int main() 
{
   vector<cd> coefficients = {1, 0, 1}; // x^2 + 1 = 0 has roots i, -i
   std::cout << find_zero(coefficients) << "\n";
   return 0;
}
```

In this code chunk, the function `poly_derivative()` is used to evaluate the derivative of our polynomial at a given point. The `find_zero()` function is updated to use the Newton's method for finding roots of a function. It continues to iterate until the absolute value of our function evaluated at the current guess gets very close to zero (within 1e-6). 

