Here is an example of how you could sort your array using C++ nested loops:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void sortArray(int arr[], int length) {
    int i, j, temp;

    // Implementing Bubble Sort
    for (i = 0; i < length-1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < length-i-1; j++) {
            // Swap if the next element is smaller
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

void printArray(int arr[], int length) {
    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
}

int main() {
    int unsorted_arr[] = {45, -21, 0, 56, 7, -47, 34, 0};
    int length = sizeof(unsorted_arr)/sizeof(unsorted_arr[0]);

    sortArray(unsorted_arr, length);
    printArray(unsorted_arr, length);

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, the function sortArray() sorts the array in ascending order using Bubble Sort technique. In Bubble Sort, we repeatedly compare the adjacent elements and swap them if they are in the wrong order. This process continues until no more swaps are needed.

The function printArray() is used to print the array elements.

The main() function initializes the unsorted array and calculates its length. It then calls sortArray() to sort the array and printArray() to print the sorted array.

