To save the output of the command that you're executing with `system()`, you can simply redirect the output to a file. This can be done by appending `> output.txt` (for standard output) or `2> errors.txt` (for error output) to your command.

But a better solution will be using `popen()` instead of `system()`, as `popen()` provides a pipe to both input and output of the command being executed.

Here's a rough example of how you might modify your code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
#include <cstdio>

