Refactored and Optimized Code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
  bool swapped;
  for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {     
    swapped = false;
    for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
        swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
    // If no two elements were swapped by inner loop, then the array is sorted.
    if (swapped == false)
      break;
  }
}

int main() {
  int arr[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
  int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
  bubbleSort(arr, n);
  cout<<"Sorted array: \n";
  for(int i = 0; i <n;i++) {
    cout << arr[i] << " ";
  }
  return 0;
}
```

Explanation of Changes and Optimizations:

The original code contains a syntax error - the swapping of elements is not handled correctly in C++. Created a temporary variable or the built-in swap() function should be used for swapping elements.

The optimized bubble sort algorithm uses a boolean variable `swapped`. This variable checks whether the inner loop resulted in any swapping of elements. If no elements were swapped in an inner loop iteration, this means that the array is already sorted and there is no need for further iterations. This optimization reduces the execution time in the best-case scenario - when the input array is already sorted - to a linear complexity (O(n)), although bubble sort is usually a quadratic complexity sorting algorithm (O(n^2)). This optimization is called "Bubble Sort Improved" or "Bubble Sort Optimized".

Remember that every sorting algorithm has a best, average, and worst-case time complexity. Even after this optimization, bubble sort is still not suitable for large data sets because its average and worst-case time complexity is O(n^2). However, this optimization certainly helps in the best case.

