Call By Value:
In this method, the value of each of the actual parameters in the calling function is copied into corresponding formal parameters of the called function. Here, the changes made to the formal parameters in the called function do not affect the values of the actual parameters in the calling function.

Call By Reference:
In this method, instead of passing the values of the actual parameters, the address of the memory locations (reference) are passed to the formal parameters of the called function. So, any changes made to the formal parameters affect the actual parameters.

In terms of efficiency and performance, call by value is generally slower, as the system needs to copy the values of the variables. Call by reference is generally faster, as only pointers are being passed around, and not the whole variables.

Here is an illustration in C++:

```C++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Call By Value
void updateValue(int value) {
    value = 100;
    cout << "Inside updateValue: " << value << endl;
}

// Call By Reference
void updateReference(int &value) {
    value = 100;
    cout << "Inside updateReference: " << value << endl;
}

int main() {
    int x = 50;

    updateValue(x); // Call By Value
    cout << "After updateValue, x: " << x << endl; // x will be still 50

    updateReference(x); // Call By Reference
    cout << "After updateReference, x: " << x << endl; // x will be 100

    return 0;
}
```
In the above code `updateValue(x)` call will not change the value of `x` in main() because it's called by value. `x` is copied to a new variable and changes are made in that local variable, not the original `x`. But `updateReference(x)` call will change the value of `x` because it's called by reference. Here, actual and formal parameters refer to the same locations, so any changes made inside the function are actually reflected in actual parameters of the caller.

