Below is a C++ code that finds the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) using Dynamic Programming. The LCS problem, which is a typical example of a problem that can be solved using dynamic programming, refers to finding the longest subsequence common to all sequences in a set of sequences (often just two sequences). 

It is to be noticed that a subsequence is defined to be a new sequence that is formed from the original sequence by deleting some (can be none at all) elements without disturbing the relative positions of the remaining elements.

```cpp

#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

// Function to find the length of Longest Common Subsequence
int LCSLength(string X, string Y) {
    int m = X.length(), n = Y.length();
    int dp[m + 1][n + 1];

    // Initialize the dp matrix 
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++)
        dp[i][0] = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)
        dp[0][i] = 0;  

    // dynamic programming 
    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (X[i - 1] == Y[j - 1])
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;

            else
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        }
    }

    return dp[m][n];
}

int main() {
    string X = "ABCBDAB", Y = "BDCAB"; 
    cout << "The length of the LCS is " << LCSLength(X, Y) << endl;
    return 0;
}

```

This Function will give the length of the longest common subsequence between two strings X and Y. The dynamic programming array, dp[][] , contains the length of the LCS at each step. The last cell of the dp table, dp[m][n] , will give us the length of the LCS of X and Y, where m and n are the lengths of of X and Y respectively.

This code above prints the length of the Longest Common Subsequence between the two strings. If you want to actually print the Longest Common Subsequence as well, you would need to add additional logic to traverse back through the dynamic programming array and formulate the actual subsequence.

