Here, the main task is to find the sum of factors of a number except the number itself. This problem can be handled by using an algorithm for finding all factors and then summing them up. In terms of efficiency, iterating from 1 to square root of n is an optimized way to find factors of n.

Here is a simple C++ program to get started:

```C++
#include<bits/stdc++.h> 
using namespace std; 

// Function to calculate sum of all proper divisors num is the given number. 
int sumOfFactors(int num) 
{ 
    // If num is 0 or 1, no factors exist excluding the number itself
    if (num == 0 || num == 1){
        return 0;
    }

    int result = 1; 

    // Find all divisors using sqrt(num), and add them 
    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(num); i++) 
    { 
        // If divisor is equals to num 
        if (num % i == 0) 
        { 
            // If both divisors are same then add it only once else add both 
            if (i == (num/i)) 
                result = result + i; 
            else
                result = result + (i + num/i); 
        } 
    } 

    // Return sum of divisors 
    return result; 
} 

// Driver function 
int main() 
{ 
    int num;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> num;
    cout << "\nSum of factors of " << num << " excluding itself is: " << sumOfFactors(num); 
    return 0; 
} 
```

This code first checks if the number is 0 or 1, if true then returns 0 because these numbers do not have any factors excluding themselves. If the number is greater than 1, it finds factors by checking divisibility from 2 to square root of the number (because a larger factor of n must be a multiple of a smaller factor that has already been checked), and if it is divisible, adds the divisor to the result. If both divisors are same, add it only once, else add both. It finally returns the sum of the factors. The time complexity of this solution is O(sqrt(n)), which is efficient for large numbers.

