q:
Implement a multithreaded version of the given C++ code. The threads should print out "Hello World" in a synchronized manner such that they don't overlap with each other. 

Identify and utilise suitable synchronization primitive(s) for this, and remember to consider and handle possible race conditions. 

Furthermore, your code should demonstrate correct thread creation, handling and termination.

Original Code:

#include <iostream> 
using namespace std; 
int main() 
{ 
  cout << "Hello World!"; 
  return 0; 
}

a:
In order to modify the above C++ program for multithreading, we use C++11's `std::thread` with a `std::mutex` for synchronization. 

Our aim here is to create multiple threads, which each print out "Hello World". The `mutex` locks the `cout` when each thread is using it, hence preventing interleaving or overlapping of the outputs from different threads.

Possible C++11 code for this multithreaded version could be:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
using namespace std;

mutex mtx;

void printHello() {
    mtx.lock();
    cout << "Hello World!" << endl;
    mtx.unlock();
}

int main() 
{ 
  const int numThreads = 10;

  thread t[numThreads];

  // Create multiple threads
  for(int i = 0 ; i < numThreads ; i++){
      t[i] = thread(printHello);
  }

  // Join the threads with the main thread
  for(int i = 0 ; i < numThreads ; i++){
      t[i].join();
  }

  return 0; 
}
```

This above program creates ten threads, each of which print "Hello World". They are synchronized via a `std::mutex` which ensures that there won't be any overlapping of the outputs.

Always remember to join your threads by using the `.join()` function. It allows the program to wait for the threads' completion before proceeding. If you forget to join a thread, it becomes a "detached thread" and its resources are automatically cleaned up when it finishes (like a daemon thread). However, resources from a joinable thread are not released until another thread calls `join` on it, so not calling `join` can result in a resource leak.

A `std::lock_guard` may also be used instead of `lock` and `unlock` functions, which provides a more exception-safe mechanism to lock and unlock a mutex in C++.
```cpp
void printHello() {
    lock_guard<mutex> guard(mtx);
    cout << "Hello World!" << endl;
}
```
Here, `mtx` will be locked when the `lock_guard` is created, and it will be automatically released when `lock_guard` goes out of scope. This is safer because if an exception is thrown within the scope of the `lock_guard`, it removes the risk of the program exiting before the mutex can be unlocked.

