Sure, here's a basic implementation of the selection sort algorithm in C++. It includes a try-catch block for handling potential invalid inputs and out-of-range exceptions. It also includes a function to reverse a sorted array and calculates the time complexity of the selection sort algorithm:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <vector>

void selectionSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    for(size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        int minIndex = i;

        for(size_t j = i+1; j < arr.size(); j++) {
            if(arr[j] < arr[minIndex])
                minIndex = j;
        }

        if (minIndex != i)
            std::swap(arr[i], arr[minIndex]);
    }
}

void reverseArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    size_t n = arr.size();

    for(size_t i = 0; i < n/2; i++)
        std::swap(arr[i], arr[n-i-1]);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> array {4, 3, 2, 10, 12, 1, 5, 6};

    try {
        auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

        selectionSort(array);

        auto stop = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

        std::cout << "Sorted array: \n";
        for(int i: array)
            std::cout << i << " ";

        reverseArray(array);
        std::cout << "\nReversed array: \n";
        for(int i: array)
            std::cout << i << " ";

        auto duration = std::chrono::duration<double>(stop - start);

        std::cout << "\nTime to sort: " << duration.count() << "s\n";
    }
    catch (const std::out_of_range& oor) {
        std::cerr << "Out of Range error: " << oor.what() << "\n";
    }
    catch(...) {
        std::cerr << "Unexpected exception caught\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

For the time complexity calculation: the timing mechanism used in this code measures the duration it takes for the function to execute and then displays the duration in seconds. Given that selection sort has a time complexity of O(n^2) in the worst case (due to the nested for loops), as the size of the array increases, the time taken for the function to execute will grow quadratically. 

Please note that the order of reversing operation is linear, i.e., O(n), where n is total number of elements present inside array.

```cpp
void rot(int* dest, const int* src, int size, int s) {
    int shift = s % size;  // Calculate the effective shift within the array size

    // Perform the rotation operation
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        dest[(i + shift) % size] = src[i];
    }
}
```

The `rot` function takes the destination array `dest`, the source array `src`, the size of the arrays `size`, and the shift value `s`. It calculates the effective shift within the array size and then performs the rotation operation by copying elements from the source array to the destination array based on the calculated shift.

