This isn't a design question, really, though it may seem like it. (Well, okay, it's kind of a design question). What I'm wondering is why the C++ `std::fstream` classes don't take a `std::string` in their constructor or open methods. Everyone loves code examples so:


#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::string filename = "testfile";      
    std::ifstream fin;

    fin.open(filename.c_str()); // Works just fine.
    fin.close();

    //fin.open(filename); // Error: no such method.
    //fin.close();
}



This gets me all the time when working with files. Surely the C++ library would use `std::string` wherever possible?

a:
The reason for this absence is historical. The `<fstream>` classes are part of the C++ Standard Library, which has evolved over time. The stream classes inherited the design from their C ancestors which handle file processing with character strings (`char*`).

The `std::fstream::open` methods were introduced in the C++98 standard, at which time `std::string` was still a new and quite a progressive feature. One of the important principles of the C++ standards is stability and compatibility with previous versions. As a result, when the library classes were modeled, the decision was made to keep them compatible with existing C-style code and libraries. Thus, users could still use C-style string literals and character pointers with the introduced file stream classes.

However, in the C++17 standard, an overload of the `open` function was finally added to `std::basic_fstream` (the template class upon which `std::fstream` is based) that accepts an `std::string` as an argument. This means that if you're using a recent compiler, the following code should now work:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::string filename = "testfile";
    std::ifstream fin;

    fin.open(filename); // C++17 onwards, now works just fine!    
    fin.close();
}
```

So, the absence of the `std::string`-overload until C++17 was primarily due to maintaining compatibility with C-oriented string handling and gradual evolution of the library in the light of approving new features. The problem has been recognized and addressed in later C++ standards.

