There are some best practices when translating between native C++ exceptions and managed exceptions in C++/CLI or C#. Here is a general approach:

1. **Catch and Rethrow:** Catch the native exceptions at the interop boundary and throw a corresponding CLI exception. There is no built-in mapping, so you'll need to map them manually. The reason behind this is that CLR exceptions cannot pass through the native/managed code boundaries, which means that a managed client cannot catch native exceptions.

2. **Meaningful Exception Translation:** While translating exceptions, be sure to include all relevant information from the original exception in the new exception. Don't lose the context. This can include the error message, error code, etc.

3. **Exception Hierarchy:** Map the native C++ exceptions to the closest .NET exception in terms of semantics. It's not usually very straightforward because the .NET framework's exception hierarchy and C++ exception classes do not have a one-to-one correspondence.

4. **Custom Exceptions:** If none of the .NET framework exception classes provide a close semantic match to the C++ exception being thrown, then consider defining a custom (user-defined) exception class. This will make your exception handling strategy more maintainable and these exceptions more catchable.

5. **Don't Swallow Exceptions Silently:** The only thing worse than an exception is an exception that got swallowed and thus leaves no trace. Unless there is a very specific reason you can safely ignore an exception, always re-throw or log exceptions.

6. **Dispose Pattern:** Consider implementing a Dispose pattern for the wrapper class to ensure the object will be cleaned up correctly, and use finaliser to catch any exception that is not caught by the user code.

Here is an example of how you might map a std::invalid_argument to an ArgumentException:

```cpp
try
{
    // Function that throws a std::invalid_argument
    // ...
}
catch (const std::invalid_argument& ex)
{
    throw gcnew System::ArgumentException(gcnew System::String(ex.what()));
}
catch (const std::exception& ex)
{
    throw gcnew System::Exception(gcnew System::String(ex.what()));
}
// Catch any other exceptions or errors...
```

This code will catch the std::invalid_argument exception (or any other standard exception), wrap its message into a .NET string, and throw a new ArgumentException (or general Exception) with that string. As stated before, the translation should be meaningful regarding the intended use of the exception.

