2. Pointer variables:
   - Function as memory addresses.
   - Can be assigned a null value.
   - Can be the target of dynamic memory allocation/deallocation.
   - Usage: When working with dynamic memory, polymorphic classes, and class instances that might be null.

In this example, we store the characters of our game in a vector of smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr<Character>`) to handle memory management. The smart pointers avoid common pitfalls and ensure proper memory management by automatically managing dynamic memory allocation and deallocation to avoid memory leaks.

When working with character instances close to the game loop, references (`const Character& character`) are used instead of pointers to emphasize that the variable must not be null and to denote no runtime cost.

