The given program is actually a simple divisor finding program, which doesn't cater to prime factorization. Therefore, we have to substantially modify it to achieve our required task. Here is a proposed solution in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int num;
    cout << "Enter Number: ";
    cin >> num;

    // Using a map to hold prime factors and their counts.
    map<int, int> factors;

    // Determine the count of 2 as a prime factor.
    while(num % 2 == 0) {
        factors[2]++;
        num /= 2;
    }

    // Now num must be odd so a skip of 2 can be used.
    for(int i = 3; i * i <= num; i += 2) {
        // while i divides num, count i and divide num.
        while(num % i == 0) {
            factors[i]++;
            num /= i;
        }
    }

    // This condition is to handle the case when num is a prime number
    // greater than 2.
    if(num > 2)
        factors[num]++;

    // Printing the factors and their counts.
    for(auto i: factors) {
        cout << i.first << '^' << i.second;
        // To not print the comma at the end.
        if(i.first != (--factors.end())->first) 
            cout << ", ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program takes a number from the user, then continuously divides that number by 2 if it's divisible to account for all factors of 2. After that, it starts from 3 and checks divisibility for only odd numbers (because even numbers won't be a factor after removing all 2's). 

After removing all the factors up to the square root of the number, if the number is still greater than 2, it means that number itself is a prime number, and it is added to the factor's map. Finally, it prints all the factors along with their counts.

