First, let's clarify what the Manhattan distance is. It is a measure of distance in a grid-based system, calculated as the sum of the absolute differences of their coordinates. For two points in 4D space, the Manhattan distance is |x1-x2| + |y1-y2| + |z1-z2| + |w1-w2|. Let's create a C++ code that calculates it:

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Point4D {
public:
    int x, y, z, w;
    Point4D() : x(0), y(0), z(0), w(0) {}
    Point4D(int x, int y, int z, int w) : x(x), y(y), z(z), w(w) {}
};

int manhattanDistance(Point4D p1, Point4D p2) {
    return abs(p1.x - p2.x) + abs(p1.y - p2.y) + abs(p1.z - p2.z) + abs(p1.w - p2.w);
}

