```cpp
#include <unordered_map>

// Declaration of std::unordered_map for memoization
std::unordered_map<int, long int> memo;

long int fibfib(int n) {
    // check n in map or not, std::unordered_map::count function will return 1 if n exist otherwise 0
    if(memo.count(n) == 1) {
        return memo[n];
    }

    long int result;

    // if n is greater than or equal to 0
    if(n >= 0) {
        // if n is less than 3
        if(n < 3) {
            result = n % 2; // n modulo 2
        } else {
            // call fibfib function recursively for n-1, n-2 and n-3 and add all the results
            result = fibfib(n - 1) + fibfib(n - 2) + fibfib(n - 3);
        }
    } else {
        // if n is less than 0, call fibfib function recursively for n+3, n+2 and n+1 and subtract results of n+2 and n+1 from n+3
        result = fibfib(n + 3) - fibfib(n + 2) - fibfib(n + 1);
    }

    // store the result in the memo map for future reference
    memo[n] = result;

    return result;
}
```
This C++ function similarly computes the FibFib sequence, applying dynamic programming using an `unordered_map` for memoization. This method effectively avoids redundant calculation for the same input `n`, and reduces time complexity. Be aware that the value type of `unordered_map` is `long int` to handle large numbers as it's unknown what values `n` can take.

