Here's the corrected and enhanced version of the code:

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<stack>
#include<string>
using namespace std;

bool correct_bracketing(string brackets) {
    stack<char> stack;
    for(char& ch : brackets) {
        switch (ch) {
        case '(':
            // An opening bracket is always accepted.
            stack.push(ch);
            break;
        case ')':
            // An closing bracket is only accepted if it can match 
            // an opening one.
            if (stack.empty() || stack.top() != '(')
                return false;
            else
                // The matching bracket is consumed.
                stack.pop();
            break;
        }
    }
    //If there's any unmatched opening bracket at the end, it's an error.
    return stack.empty();
}

int main(){
    cout<<correct_bracketing("()")<<endl;
    cout<<correct_bracketing("()()()()()")<<endl;
    cout<<correct_bracketing(")(")<<endl;
    cout<<correct_bracketing("(")<<endl;
    return 0;
}
```

How the function works: 
Creates an stack object. 
Iterates over every character of the string.
If the character '(' is found, pushes it into the stack. 
If the character ')' is found, checks that the stack is not empty and top of the stack is '('. 

If both these conditions aren't met, an opening bracket isn't matched with a closing bracket, thus function returns false.  If the condition is true, pops the stack.

Finally, if the stack isn't empty at the end, it means we have unmatched opening brackets left, which means the string is not properly nested or bracketed, thus function returns false. If stack is empty, function returns true. 

