The edit distance or Levenshtein distance between two strings is the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions, deletions, or substitutions) required to change one string into the other.

Here's a simple C++ implementation that uses dynamic programming:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int editDistance(std::string str1, std::string str2)
{
    int m = str1.length();
    int n = str2.length();

    std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(m + 1, std::vector<int>(n + 1));

    for(int i = 0; i <= m; i++)
    {
        for(int j = 0; j <= n; j++)
        {
            // If first string is empty, insert all characters of second string into first
            if(i == 0)
                dp[i][j] = j;

            // If second string is empty, remove all characters of first string
            else if(j == 0)
                dp[i][j] = i;

            // If last characters are same, ignore last character and get count for remaining strings
            else if(str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1])
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];

            // If last characters are not same, consider all possibilities and find minimum
            else 
                dp[i][j] = 1 + std::min({dp[i][j - 1],   // Insert in str1
                                         dp[i - 1][j],   // Remove from str1
                                         dp[i - 1][j - 1] // Replace in str1
                                         });
        }
    }

    return dp[m][n];
}

int main()
{
    std::string str1 = "kitten";
    std::string str2 = "sitting";
    std::cout << "Edit distance between " << str1 << " and " << str2 << " is " << editDistance(str1, str2) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation creates a 2D dynamic programming table where dp[i][j] holds the edit distance between str1[0..i-1] and str2[0..j-1]. It fills the table in a top-down manner and finally, the bottom right corner of the table contains the edit distance between the input strings. The time complexity is O(m*n), where m and n are the lengths of the input strings.

Sure, it's important to note that C++ Standard Library does not provide a direct implementation for semaphores. However, we can construct a semaphore using other synchronization primitives like `std::mutex` and `std::condition_variable`.

Here is a simple implementation of a semaphore class in C++:

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class Semaphore {
    private:
        std::mutex mutex_;
        std::condition_variable condition_;
        size_t count_;

    public:
        Semaphore(size_t count = 0)
            : count_(count) {
        }

        void notify() {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            ++count_;
            condition_.notify_one();
        }

        void wait() {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            while(count_ == 0) {
                condition_.wait(lock);
            }
            --count_;
        }

        bool try_wait() {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            if(count_) {
                --count_;
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        }
};
```

In the code above, the `wait` function will block if the count is zero and will continue blocking until another thread calls the `notify` function. The `notify` function increments the count and wakes up one of the waiting threads, if any exist. The `try_wait` function tries to decrement the count and returns immediately whether it was successful or not.

You can then use this semaphore in your C++ code as you would use any other semaphore.

