A more optimized approach would be to use the properties of numbers divisible by 11 and 13 and base 10's property to calculate the number of occurrences of '7' in numbers less than n divided by 11 or 13 but not by both. This is because any number A can be written as A = A//10 * 10 + A%10 where '//' is the integer division operator, which separates the last digit from the rest of the number. We'll need to calculate the number of times digit '7' occurred in the last digit and the rest of the number separately and then add them up.

