To specialize `std::swap()`, you can create your own overload in the same namespace as your custom type. This will be picked up through Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) when std containers are using this.

Let's take a simple class `Foo` for example:
```c++
namespace Custom {
    class Foo {
        // Initial class definition
    };
}
```
You can specialize `std::swap()` in the same namespace:
```c++
namespace Custom {
    void swap(Foo& a, Foo& b) noexcept {
        // Custom swap for objects of class 'Foo'
    }
}
```
When your `Foo` type is used in std containers, the custom swap will be invoked because of the ADL.

Note that when specializing or overloading function templates from the std namespace, you should always ensure that your specialization or overload has exactly the same behavior as the original — otherwise, you are likely to get a program that compiles but behaves incorrectly. That's why using the fully featured custom `swap()` is recommended.

Please note that you cannot specialize `std::swap()` directly since it is located in the `std` namespace. Doing so would violate the rules of the standard library — you cannot add overloads to `std`.

If you want to specialize `std::swap()` for a class you created, you should create a custom `swap()` function in the same namespace as your class. The `std` functions will pick that up.

