Here's a template of C++ function to achieve string permutation but this may not suitable for running on large inputs:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <unordered_set>

using namespace std;

void permute(string a, int l, int r, unordered_set<string>& res) {
    if (l == r) {
        res.insert(a);
    }
    else {
        for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
            swap(a[l], a[i]);
            permute(a, l + 1, r, res);
            swap(a[l], a[i]);
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    string str = "XYZWXYZ";
    unordered_set<string> res;
    permute(str, 0, str.size() - 1, res);
    for (string s : res) {
        cout << s << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n!) and it's the best possible because you have to generate n! permutations for a string of length n. However, note that C++ has a recursion depth limit, which means this code will fail for large input string (more than ~1000 characters) due to a recursion stack overflow.

For larger strings, you may need to consider alternatives to recursion such as using a lot of memory to store intermediate results or using a customized,generative algorithm that doesn't solely rely on recursion. Note that generating all unique permutations for strings of such a length may still consume a substantial amount of memory, and may not be practical for a normal pc.

