Early Binding and Late Binding are both concepts related to how C++ connections are made between function calls and their definitions, or more generally, how names are associated with entities.

Early Binding (also known as static binding or compile-time binding)
This strategy resolves all types, function calls, variable calls, etc., at compile-time. As a result, templates and overloading operator and function are typical applications. Here's an example:

```C++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Rectangle {
public:
    void Area(int length, int breadth) {
        cout << "Area: " << length * breadth << endl;
    }
};
int main() {
    Rectangle rect;
    rect.Area(4, 5);
    return 0;
}
```
In this simple example, the `Area` function of class `Rectangle` uses early binding. At compile time, the compiler understands what is rect, how to call `Area`, and what type its parameters should be.

Benefits: Compile-time error checking, efficiency (since no extra overhead at runtime), simpler to implement.

Drawbacks: Flexibility is limited because everything needs to be determined at compile-time.

Late Binding (also known as dynamic binding or runtime binding)
This strategy resolves function calls at runtime, which makes this ideal for achieving polymorphism. Virtual functions are typical applications. Here's an example:

```C++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Rectangle {
public:
    virtual void Area() {
        cout << "Area from Rectangle" << endl;
    }
};
class Triangle : public Rectangle {
public:
    void Area() override {
        cout << "Area from Triangle" << endl;
    }
};
int main() {
    Rectangle* rect = new Triangle();
    rect->Area(); // This will call Triangle's Area at runtime
    delete rect;
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `Area` function uses late binding. Even though `rect` is a `Rectangle*`, the actual object it points to is an instance of `Triangle`. So, `rect->Area()` will call `Triangle`'s `Area` – this decision is made at runtime.

Benefits: Greater flexibility since you can bind function calls based on the actual objects at runtime.

Drawbacks: Small overhead since binding happens at runtime, absence of early error checking.

Note: C++ uses early binding by default. We have to explicitly specify using the `virtual` keyword for late binding.

In summary, you choose early or late binding depending on your requirements. If you need efficiency and don't require polymorphism, then early binding is better. Otherwise, if your design involves inheritance and you want to take advantage of polymorphism, then late binding is the way to go.

