    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Joint>> expected = {
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(0),
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(0),
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(0),
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(1.5708),
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(1.5708),
        std::make_shared<RevoluteJoint>(-1.5708)
    };

    // Call the IK solver and obtain the result
    // Assume result is populated with the computed joint configurations

    // Compare the result with the expected joint configurations
    if (compareJointConfigurations(result, expected)) {
        std::cout << "Result matches expected joint configurations" << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Result does not match expected joint configurations" << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In the solution, the `compareJointConfigurations` function iterates through the result and expected joint configurations, comparing the angles of corresponding joints. If any pair of angles differs by more than a small tolerance (1e-6 in this case), the function returns false, indicating a mismatch. Otherwise, it returns true, signifying that the result matches the expected joint configurations. The main function demonstrates how to use this comparison function with the provided result and expected joint configurations.

