Propose an optimization for the following recursive function in C++, which searches for a target value within a binary search tree. Bear in mind the traversal of the tree and efficiency of target value comparison. The current implementation uses in-order traversal.

Here is the recursive function:

bool searchValue(Node* root, int target) { 
    if (root == NULL) {
        return false;    
    }
    else if (root->data == target) {
        return true;    
    }
    else {
        bool res1 = searchValue(root->left, target); 
        if(res1) return true; 
        bool res2 = searchValue(root->right, target); 
        return res2;      
    }
}

a:
The current implementation searches both left and right subtrees irrespective of the target value, which is inefficient considering the properties of a binary search tree (BST). In a BST, all nodes in the left subtree are less than the root, and all nodes in the right subtree are greater than the root. Using this property, we can optimize the search by choosing the correct subtree to explore based on whether the target value is less or greater than the root's data. If the target value equals the root's data, we've found our node.

Here is the optimized function:

```cpp
bool searchValue(Node* root, int target) { 
    if (root == NULL) {
        return false;    
    }
    if (root->data == target) {
        return true;
    }

    // If target is less than root's data, recur for left subtree
    if (root->data > target) {
        return searchValue(root->left, target); 
    }
    // If target is greater than root's data, recur for right subtree
    else {
        return searchValue(root->right, target);
    }
}
```
This optimized function reduces the search space by half at each step, improving performance. The best case time complexity is O(1) (when the root is the target) and the worst case time complexity is O(h) (where h is the height of the tree). For a balanced tree, this is approximates to O(log n) where n is the number of nodes in the tree.

