Aspect-oriented programming (AOP) increases modularity by allowing the separation of cross-cutting concerns. It includes programming methods and tools that support the modularization of concerns at the level of source code, while "aspects" can change the behavior of the base code (the non-aspect part of a program) by applying advice (additional behavior) at various join points (points in a program) specified in a quantification or query called a pointcut.

However, C++ lacks native feature support for Aspect-Oriented Programming. To solve your problem, you can use AspectC++: an aspect-oriented extension to the C++ programming language. Using this extension we can describe cross-cutting concerns, intercept and modify behaviors of methods. Nevertheless, in this problem-solving contemplation, we'll demonstrate a minimalistic custom approach for simplicity.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>
using namespace std;

class Observer {
public:
    virtual void onAction() = 0;
};

class MethodInterceptionAspect {
    vector<Observer*> observers;

public:
    void addObserver(Observer &o) {
        observers.push_back(&o);
    }

    void Action() {
        for(auto &o: observers)
            o->onAction();
    }
};

class LoggingAspect: public Observer {
public:
    virtual void onAction() {
        cout << "Log: Action performed" << endl;
    }
};

class ModificationAspect: public Observer {
public:
    virtual void onAction() {
        cout << "Modifier: Action performed" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    MethodInterceptionAspect aspect;
    LoggingAspect logging;
    ModificationAspect modification;

    aspect.addObserver(logging);
    aspect.addObserver(modification);
    aspect.Action();

    return 0;
}
```

This code demonstrates a simplified AOP approach. 

The `Observer` class acts as the base for an aspect that wants to intercept method execution. 

The `MethodInterceptionAspect` class lets aspects register themselves for method interception and triggers interested aspects when a method is to be intercepted.

The `LoggingAspect` and `ModificationAspect` are the cross-cutting concerns, intercepting method execution for logging and behavioral modification purposes respectively.

Advantages of this approach:
- It enhances modularity by enabling the separation of cross-cutting concerns.
- It allows altering the behavior of classes in a clean and maintainable way.

Limitations/Challenges with this approach:
- C++ doesn't support AOP natively, so developer have to come up with custom solutions.
- Given the lack of language support, the implementation might get complex for large programs.
- AOP paradigm is relatively less common, hence more developer training would be required.
- Debugging can be challenging due to the indirect nature of AOP execution paths.

This approach might not suit every problem, it is good to be aware and understand the approach as it can be useful in certain scenarios such as drawing boundaries, or for testing. For most of the problems which involve inheritance and polymorphism, an OOP can be generally more suitable and easier to handle. It's all about choosing the right tool for the particular problem.

