Below is a basic implementation of the Observer pattern in C++. This implementation assumes that the observers don't need any specific data when a change occurs. If they do, you'll need to expand this implementation accordingly.

```cpp
#include <vector>

class AbstractObserver
{
public:
    virtual void Update() = 0;
};

class AbstractSubject
{
public:
    virtual void RegisterObserver(AbstractObserver* observer) = 0;
    virtual void RemoveObserver(AbstractObserver* observer) = 0;
    virtual void NotifyObservers() = 0;
};

class ConcreteSubject : public AbstractSubject
{
private:
    std::vector<AbstractObserver*> m_Observers;

public:
    void RegisterObserver(AbstractObserver* observer) override
    {
        m_Observers.push_back(observer);
    }

    void RemoveObserver(AbstractObserver* observer) override
    {
        m_Observers.erase(std::remove(m_Observers.begin(), m_Observers.end(), observer), m_Observers.end());
    }

    void NotifyObservers() override
    {
        for (AbstractObserver* observer : m_Observers)
        {
            observer->Update();
        }
    }
};

class ConcreteObserver : public AbstractObserver
{
public:
    void Update() override
    {
        // Do something when notified of a change.
    }
};
```

In this code, `AbstractObserver` is the Observer Class and `AbstractSubject` is the Subject Class. `ConcreteObserver` and `ConcreteSubject` are example of Concrete Implementations of these abstract classes.

The Observer Class declares an `Update` method that gets called when the Subject changes.

The Subject Class keeps track of all its observers and provides methods to add or remove observers. It also has a `NotifyObservers` method that gets called whenever a change happens to the Subject. This method, in turn, calls the `Update` method on all registered observers.

To use this code, you create a ConcreteSubject instance, add ConcreteObserver instances as observers to the ConcreteSubject and then call `NotifyObservers` on the ConcreteSubject instance whenever appropriate.

Remember that the specific use cases for this pattern depend on your application requirements. Always consider whether the Observer pattern is the best fit for your needs before applying it to your code.

