I'm writing a templated class, and I want to have a `tuple` that holds some data. The type of the `tuple` is related to the template arguments in this way:

template<typename ...Types>
class MyClass{
     public:
          std::tuple<int, (repeat "int" sizeof...(Types) times)> MyData;
}


For example, `MyClass<int, float, std::string, double>` would result in a `MyData` variable of type `std::tuple<int, int, int, int>`. I've looked into fold expressions, but I'm not sure that they can do what I want. Is there a way to do this in C++, and if so, how?

a:
In C++, these kind of metaprogramming tasks can be achieved by utilizing a helper structure with template specialization (or, in modern C++, std::type_identity). The idea is to create a type generator that maps your types to an int, generates type packs and packs them into a tuple:

Here is a workable solution:

```cpp
#include <tuple>

template<typename ...Types>
struct type_to_int {
    typedef std::tuple<int> type;
};

template<typename T, typename ...Types>
struct type_to_int<T, Types...> {
    typedef decltype(std::tuple_cat(std::declval<std::tuple<int>>(), std::declval<typename type_to_int<Types...>::type>())) type;
};

template<typename ...Types>
class MyClass
{
public:
    typename type_to_int<Types...>::type MyData;
}

int main() {
    MyClass<int, double, std::string> c;  // this will be std::tuple<int, int, int>
    return 0;
}
```

In C++20, the solution can be simplified with `std::type_identity_t` and template parameter packs:

```cpp
template<typename ...Types>
class MyClass{
public:
    std::tuple<int, std::type_identity_t<int, Types>...> MyData;
};
```

Each type in `Types...` pack will create a separate `int` due to the way `std::type_identity_t` works. After that, the expansion into the tuple will give us the desired result.

