using namespace std;

// Function to implement Euclidean algorithm
int gcd(int a, int b)
{
    // ensure that a is always greater than b
    if (a < b)
        swap(a, b);

    while (b != 0)
    {
        int remainder = a % b;

        // the gcd of a and b is the same as the gcd of b and remainder
        a = b;
        b = remainder;
    }

    return a;
}

int main() {
    int a, b;
    cout << "Enter two numbers: ";

    // ensure that the entered parameters are integers
    if (!(cin >> a >> b)) 
    {
        cerr << "Error: Please enter an integer input." << endl;
        return -1;
    }

    cout << "The gcd of " << a << " and " << b << " is " << gcd(a, b) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In the above code:

1. We have a `gcd` function. The function starts by swapping `a` and `b` if `a < b` to ensure `a` is always greater than `b`.
2. Then it runs a while loop until `b != 0`. Inside the loop, it finds the remainder of `a / b`, assigns the value of `b` to `a`, and the value of the remainder to `b`.
3. As per the Euclidean algorithm, `gcd(a, b)` is equivalent to `gcd(b, remainder)`. The loop continues until `b = 0`, at which point `a` contains the GCD of the original `a` and `b`.

