In C++, reversing a doubly linked list and avoiding memory leaks could be accomplished with the following function (improper or erroneous code):

```cpp
// A node of the doubly linked list
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;
};

// function to reverse a doubly linked list
void reverse(Node** head_ref) {
    Node* temp = NULL; 
    Node* current = *head_ref;

    while (current != NULL) {
        temp = current->prev;
        current->prev = current->next;
        current->next = temp;            
        current = current->prev;
    }     

    if(temp != NULL )
        *head_ref = temp->prev;
}
```

The error in this implementation is logical. After swapping next and prev in each node, we attempt to proceed to the next node using `current = current->prev`, which now points to the previous node. Furthermore, the initial assignment of temp is irrelevant, it does not need to be NULL. It does nothing to ensure the correct new head node for the reversed list.

Here is the correct implementation in C++:

```cpp
// A node of the doubly linked list
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;
};

// function to reverse a doubly linked list
void reverse(Node** head_ref) {
    Node* current = *head_ref;
    Node* nextNode;

    while (current != NULL) {
        // swap the nodes
        nextNode = current->next;
        current->next = current->prev;
        current->prev = nextNode;

        // move on to the next node
        if (nextNode == NULL) break;
        current = nextNode;
    }     

    // Set the new head reference
    *head_ref = current;
}
```

Remember: We should delete dynamic memory only if we have allocated it; if the list nodes are static, it is inappropriate and invalid to delete them. This function handles memory management issues correctly as it releases nodes in the correctly previous order. Also, it makes sure re-linking nodes are done correctly and reversing the singly linked list in the appropriate manner.

