The time complexity of bubble sort is O(n^2), where n is the number of elements to sort. The space complexity is O(1), which means it is a space-efficient algorithm. But this complexity makes it inefficient on larger lists, and ordinarily inefficient in relation to efficient sort algorithms like quicksort, mergesort, or heapsort.

Bubble sort can be optimized by stopping the algorithm if the inner loop didn't cause any swap. This means that if we go through the whole list and there’s no element to swap, the list is already sorted and we can skip the rest of the iterations: 

