int binarySearch(int data[], int size, int key) {
    int min = 0;
    int max = size - 1;

    while (min <= max) {
        int mid = min + (max - min) / 2; // calculate the midpoint for roughly equal partition

        if (data[mid] == key) {
            return mid; // key found at index mid
        } else if (data[mid] < key) {
            min = mid + 1; // key is in the upper half
        } else {
            max = mid - 1; // key is in the lower half
        }
    }

    return -1; // key not found
}
```

The `binarySearch` function initializes `min` to 0 and `max` to `size - 1`, representing the range of the array to be searched. It then enters a while loop that continues until the `min` and `max` pointers converge. Within the loop, the midpoint `mid` is calculated using the formula `(min + max) / 2`. If the element at `mid` matches the `key`, the function returns `mid`. If the element at `mid` is less than the `key`, the search range is adjusted to the upper half of the array. If the element at `mid` is greater than the `key`, the search range is adjusted to the lower half of the array. If the loop completes without finding the `key`, the function returns -1 to indicate that the `key` is not present in the array.

