ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {

    ListNode temp(INT_MIN); // create a temporary dummy node
    ListNode* tail = &temp; // keep track of the last tail so we can attach new nodes here

    // loop till either of l1 or l2 becomes null
    while(l1 && l2){

        //if the value in l1 is smaller, attach it to the tail, else attach l2 to tail
        if(l1->val < l2->val){
            tail->next = l1;
            l1 = l1->next;
        } else {
            tail->next = l2;
            l2 = l2->next;
        }

        //after attaching go to next tail
        tail = tail->next;  
    }

    // if any of the lists is still non empty, that means it is already sorted and can be attached as it is to the tail
    tail->next = l1? l1 : l2;

    //return the next of dummy node which is the start of the new sorted list
    return temp.next;   
}
```

Just to clarify, in the provided solution `temp` variable is a dummy node, which is placed before the head of merged sorted list. `Tail` variable follows the last node in merged list. It starts from `temp` and with each iteration shifts to the next added node.

`temp.next` in the end stands for the head of the merged sorted list. `INT_MIN` is used to initialize dummy node. It stands for the smallest possible integer. Any number from our lists will be greater, so first element will be placed after dummy node.

