C++ provides different options for storing key-value pairs. The most common is to use STL map or unordered_map. Here's the basic usage of each:

1. std::map: 

This internally implements a self-balancing red black tree. The time complexity for search, erase and insert operations is log(n).

```cpp
#include <map>

int main() {
    std::map<int, int> KeyValuePairs;

    // Inserting key-value pairs into the map
    KeyValuePairs[1] = 100;
    KeyValuePairs[2] = 200;
    KeyValuePairs[3] = 300;

    // Accessing value using key
    int value = KeyValuePairs[2];  // value will be 200

    // Erasing a key-value pair
    KeyValuePairs.erase(1);

    return 0;
}
```

2. std::unordered_map:

This internally implements a hash table. The time complexity for search, erase and insert operations can be assumed to be constant O(1) on average.

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>

int main() {
    std::unordered_map<int, int> KeyValuePairs;

    // Inserting key-value pairs into the map
    KeyValuePairs[1] = 100;
    KeyValuePairs[2] = 200;
    KeyValuePairs[3] = 300;

    // Accessing value using key
    int value = KeyValuePairs[2];  // value will be 200

    // Erasing a key-value pair
    KeyValuePairs.erase(1);

    return 0;
}
```

Which one to choose depends on the specific requirements and constraints of the problem. Usually, if order is important, std::map is preferred. If the order is not important, and faster access time is required, std::unordered_map is preferred.

