Quick sort is a well-known sorting technique based on the divide-and-conquer strategy. Here is a brief parallel version implementation which uses `OpenMP` in `C++`.

```C++
#include <omp.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

int partition(vector<int>& array, int low, int high) {
    int x = array[high];
    int i = (low - 1);

    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (array[j] <= x) {
            i++;
            swap(array[i], array[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(array[i + 1], array[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}

void quickSort(vector<int>& array, int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot = partition(array, low, high);

        #pragma omp task firstprivate(array, low, pivot)
        {
            quickSort(array, low, pivot - 1);
        }
        #pragma omp task firstprivate(array, high, pivot)
        {
            quickSort(array, pivot + 1, high);
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    vector<int> array = {10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70};

    #pragma omp parallel
    {
        #pragma omp single
        {
            quickSort(array, 0, array.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    // print sorted array
    for(int i = 0; i < array.size(); i++) {
        cout << array[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that if you're using a `gcc` compiler, you must compile this with `-fopenmp` flag to enable OpenMP.  

This version of parallel quick sort uses two tasks for each recursive call. Each task gets its own copy of the array, which can lead to a large amount of memory being used. It's also worth to note that, in practice parallelism is not always beneficial for small inputs due to the overhead of thread creation, and some sort of limit on recursion depth might be useful. 

However, in general, the QuickSort algorithm can greatly benefit from parallel execution. The main idea behind the parallel version of QuickSort is to perform the partitioning step in parallel.

