You can prevent a class from being allocated on the heap (i.e., prevent the use of the `new` operator) by making the class's `operator new` function private. This way, the compiler will give an error if someone tries to create an instance of the class on the heap.

Here is an example:

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { /* constructor code */ }
    ~MyClass() { /* destructor code */ }

    // Other public member functions
    // ...

private:
    // Prevent use of 'new' by making operator new private
    static void* operator new(size_t) = delete;
    static void* operator new[](size_t) = delete;
};
```

The `new` and `new[]` operators are deleted to prevent heap allocation, if someone tries to create an instance of `MyClass` on the heap, he will get a compilation error. 

```cpp
