In C++, as a command-line primarily text-based interface, there's no native support for gradient coloring, mainly because the Simple Text Output Protocol does not support it. Hence gradient color in a console is quite difficult to implement like in web development or GUI-based development and C++ console applications may not be the perfect choice to handle such requirements.

However, you can change colors, including text color and background color in the console output, using ANSI escape codes or Windows specific API calls. 

For instance, using ANSI escape codes to change color (works in Unix systems):

```C++
#include <iostream>

#define RESET "\033[0m"
#define BLACK "\033[30m"      /* Black */
#define RED "\033[31m"        /* Red */
#define GREEN "\033[32m"      /* Green */
#define YELLOW "\033[33m"     /* Yellow */
#define BLUE "\033[34m"       /* Blue */

int main() {
    std::cout << RED << "This is red text." << RESET << std::endl;
    std::cout << GREEN << "This is green text." << RESET << std::endl;
    return 0; 
}
```

If you are particularly looking for Windows systems, unfortunately, the console does not support ANSI codes out of the box until you turn on the Virtual Terminal processing. Here's an approach for Windows systems using Windows specific API, but it's not much flexible:

```C++
#include <Windows.h> 
#include <iostream> 

int main()
{
    HANDLE  hConsole;
    hConsole = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);

    SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_RED | FOREGROUND_INTENSITY);
    std::cout << "This is red text." << std::endl;

    SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_GREEN| FOREGROUND_INTENSITY);
    std::cout << "This is green text."<< std::endl;

    return 0;
}


```  

However, you can't set color gradients in the console, since the color model for text consoles is pretty limited. You can fake it by printing out sequences of solid color text by changing the color after each char or string, but actual gradients aren't possible with a purely text-based console.

If you truly need a gradient, it would be more appropriate to use a more advanced terminal (i.e., Windows Terminal, advanced Unix terminals, etc.) that supports outputting actual full-color images, or switching to a proper graphical user interface (GUI) environment for your application.

