1. Create a set from every character in each word (ignoring letter case)  - `set(word.lower())`

2. Count how many unique characters each word has - `len(set(word.lower()))`

3. Find the maximum number of unique characters among all words - `max(len(set(word.lower())) for word in words)`

4. Filter all words that have a number of unique characters that equals to the maximum - `(word for word in words if len(set(word.lower())) == max(len(set(word.lower())) for word in words))`

5. From the filtered words, return the 'smallest' word when comparing all words as lowercase - `min(.., key=str.lower))`

The equivalent code in C++ would be:

```c++
#include <algorithm>
#include <set>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// Helper function to return number of unique characters in a string
int uniqueChars(const std::string& str) {
    std::set<int> unique(str.begin(), str.end());
    return unique.size();
}

std::string find_max(const std::vector<std::string>& words) {
    // convert all characters to lowercase
    std::vector<std::string> lower_words(words.size());
    std::transform(words.begin(), words.end(), lower_words.begin(), [](std::string str) {
        std::transform(str.begin(), str.end(), str.begin(), ::tolower);
        return str;
    });

    // get the maximum number of unique characters
    int max_unique_chars = 0;
    for(const std::string& word: lower_words)
        max_unique_chars = std::max(max_unique_chars, uniqueChars(word));

    // find the word with max_unique_chars unique characters and the smallest lexicographical order
    return *std::min_element(lower_words.begin(), lower_words.end(), [max_unique_chars](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
        if (uniqueChars(a) == max_unique_chars && uniqueChars(b) == max_unique_chars)
            return a < b;
        return uniqueChars(a) > uniqueChars(b);
    });
}
```
This C++ version is a bit lengthy because the Python version was making use of some very compact and high-level language features.

And it is important to note that lexicographical comparison in C++ uses ASCII codes where uppercase letters have a smaller code than lowercase ones, which is the opposite of Python's str.lower comparison. Therefore, converting all strings to lowercase for comparison makes sure we get the equivalent Python output.

