Below is a simple C++ code snippet for computing the disparity between two floating-point numbers a and b. It features precision consideration and handles underflow and overflow scenarios:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
#include <cmath>

double computeDisparity(double a, double b) {
    double result = a - b;

    if (std::isinf(result)) {
        std::cerr << "Overflow encountered.\n";
        return std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    }

    if (result != 0 && std::abs(result) < std::numeric_limits<double>::min()) {
        std::cerr << "Underflow encountered.\n";
        return 0;
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    double a, b;
    std::cout << "Enter two floating point numbers:\n";
    std::cin >> a >> b;

    // set the decimal precision to 10
    std::cout.precision(10);

    double disparity = computeDisparity(a, b);

    if (!std::isinf(disparity)) {
        std::cout << "The disparity between " << a << " and " << b << " is " << disparity << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, if the result of the calculation is infinity, an overflow occurred. If the absolute value of the result is less than the smallest representable positive number, an underflow occurred. In either case, the code prints a diagnostic message and returns a value that reflects the error condition.

