Here is a basic concept of a program in C++ to implement a queue and clear operation.

C++ provides queue data structure as a part of standard template library (STL). The operations on queue like push (insert in the first) and pop (remove from the first) that have time complexity of O(1).

However, clearing the queue seems to take linear time with current stl implementation as we are popping the elements out one by one.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
#include<queue>
#include<cstdlib>
#include<ctime>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    srand(time(0));
    queue <int> q;
    int n = rand() % 100;

    // Enqueue operation
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        q.push(rand() % 1000);
    }

    // Check if queue is empty
    if(q.empty()) {
        cout << "Queue is empty." << endl;
        return 1;
    } else {
        cout << "Queue is not empty. Proceeding for clearing queue." << endl;
    }

    clock_t begin = clock();

    // Clearing the queue using pop operation
    while(!q.empty()) {
        q.pop();
    }
    clock_t end = clock();
    double time_spent = double(end - begin) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
    cout << "Time complexity for clearing the queue is: " << time_spent << " seconds." << endl;

    if(q.empty()) {
        cout << "Queue is now empty." << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "There are still elements in the queue." << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Instead of popping elements one by one, we directly initialize q with empty queue (q = queue<int>()) which can clear the queue in O(1) time. However, this is not a guaranteed time complexity and the actual time complexity depends heavily on implementation details of STL.

```cpp
// Clearing the queue using direct initialization with empty queue
q = queue<int>();
```

Note: Keep in mind that while(rand() % n) generates random number between 0 and n-1 inclusive, but it doesn’t generate high quality randomness. It is often used for brevity in simple demonstration programs like the one above. For more serious pseudo-random number generation requirements, use a good library/class dedicated for this purpose. 

Further improvement and exception handling will depend on requirement as it varies from project to project. Exception handling in C++ is a large topic in itself. 

For user interaction, clear and proper messages should be used. We have used cout for that in this simple demonstration program. UI library or framework should be used for full fledged UI.

In terms of performance improvement, it really depends on the specifics of how your queue is being used. 

There are also advanced data structures specifically optimized for queuing / dequeuing operations, which could well make operations faster if performance is an issue. 

You might also look into threading and concurrent queue structures if you are experiencing performance issues and your application can be broken into separate parts that can run simultaneously.

