Binary search is an efficient searching algorithm that works on sorted datasets. This algorithm divides the array into two halves and compares the value to be searched with the middle element of the array. If the value to be searched is found, the middle index is returned. If the value is less than the middle element, the binary search is performed on the left half of the array. If the value is greater than the middle element, the search is performed on the right half. The process continues until the value is found or the range of indices is exhausted.

Here's one of the most efficient ways of writing a Binary Search function in C++:

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int binarySearch(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = nums.size() - 1;
    while(left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if(nums[mid] == target)
            return mid;
        else if(nums[mid] < target)
            left = mid+1;
        else
            right = mid-1;
    }
    return -1; // If not found
}
```

In the above algorithm, it takes logarithmic search time making it very efficient even for large-sized data inputs.

You can also make use of C++ STL library function lower_bound and upper_bound for binary searches which internally uses binary search to give output in logarithm time which is a quite optimal solution for large inputs.

