In C++, global variables are initialized before the main() function is executed. The initialization order of global variables in different compilation units is not defined. However, within a single compilation unit, initialization order follows the order of definition.

Global variables are indeed zero-initialized before any other initialization takes place. This is called static initialization and it happens before dynamic initialization. It means that built-in types like `int`, `float` and `pointer` are initialized to zero, and class types are zero-initialized which means that their default constructor is called. This static initialization ensures that your global variables like pointers are not filled with garbage.

Therefore in your case:

MySingleton* MySingleton::_instance = NULL;

This NULL pointer initialization will happen before main() is started. Following any singleton pattern will ensure there will be only one instance.

But you should be cautious about initialization order when your program grows. If some other global object tries to access MySingleton::_instance during its initialization (before main()), it could find it not properly initialized yet. Because as mentioned earlier, C++ doesn't guarantee the initialization order of global/static objects across different translation units (different .cpp files). 

This is a well-known problem called Static Initialization Order Fiasco. One of the available solutions is use of a function local static instance (Meyers' Singleton) instead of a global static variable. The static local variables are initialized when control first passes through their declaration (not before main()), which eliminates the initialization order fiasco:

```c++
MySingleton& MySingleton::instance()
{
    static MySingleton theInstance;
    return theInstance;
}
```

