But, complex types like classes or structures can have different sizes because C++ allows additional features which don't exist in C, like member functions, virtual functions, etc.

Also, padding and alignment can impact the total size of complex types. Compilers try to optimize the data by aligning it to the word size of the machine. This can result in 'padding' (adding extra bytes), so the size can be bigger than the sum of its individual members.

