Here is the C++ program using the bubble sort algorithm and then we will optimize it as well. The unoptimized bubble sort has a time complexity of O(n^2) which makes it inefficient for large data sets. Further, I will add tracking of the original indexes.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(vector<int> &arr, vector<int> &idx) {
    int n = arr.size();

    // Initialize original indexes
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx[i] = i;

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
                swap(idx[j], idx[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    vector<int> arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    vector<int> idx(arr.size());

    bubbleSort(arr, idx);

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    cout << endl;

    for (int i = 0; i < idx.size(); i++) {
        cout << idx[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This code sorts the integer list as well as maintains a list of original indices in the sorted list. Let's enhance it further by using an efficient sorting algorithm.

We'll use the standard C++ sort function because it employs an algorithm called IntroSort which is a hybrid sorting algorithm derived from QuickSort and HeapSort. This sorting algorithm will perform much faster than BubbleSort, having an average and worst-case time complexity of O(n log n).

