The provided code does not handle the transitive property correctly. If a<b and b<c, a must also be less than c, but the given code is not accounting for this, it just checks for pairwise comparisons of adjacent elements.

We can correct this by modifying the code so that it checks every possible combination of two numbers in the list, not just adjacent elements. 

Here is how it could be implemented in C++:

```C++
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
vector <pair<int, int>> transitive_closure(vector<int> integers) {
    vector <pair<int, int>> pairs;
    sort(integers.begin(), integers.end());
    for(int i = 0; i < integers.size(); i++)
        for(int j = i+1; j < integers.size(); j++)
            pairs.push_back(make_pair(integers.at(i), integers.at(j)));
    return pairs;
}
int main() {
    vector<int> integers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    vector <pair<int, int>> pairs = transitive_closure(integers);
    for(int i = 0; i < pairs.size(); i++)
        cout << "(" << pairs.at(i).first << ", " << pairs.at(i).second << ") ";
    return 0;
}
```
The transitive property of the less than relation is now properly handled by ensuring that for each pair (a, b), if a < b, then we have included a pair (a, c) for every c > b. Also, we sort the integers at the beginning which also handles the property of transitivity where if a<b and b<c, then a<c.

