The break statement in C++ has the capability to terminate the loop or switch-case construct in which it is placed. Essentially, it "breaks" out of the construct, hence the name "break". Let's see how it can be used in both loops and switch-case constructs.

Loops:

In looping constructs such as for, while, and do-while, the break statement is often used to exit prematurely. This might be because a certain condition has been met and further iteration is no longer necessary. Here is an example with a for loop:

```c++
for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    cout << i << endl;
    if(i == 5) {
        break;
    }
}
```

In this code, the loop would normally iterate from 0 to 9. However, because of the break statement, the loop terminates when 'i' equals to 5. So, the output will only include numbers from 0 to 5.

Switch-case constructs:

In switch-case constructs, a break statement is used to end the execution of a particular case. It prevents the "fallthrough" behavior, where without a break, the program would continue to execute the next case even if the evaluated expression doesn't match it. Here is an example:

```c++
int x = 2;
switch(x) {
    case 1:
        cout << "x is 1";
        break;
    case 2: 
        cout << "x is 2";
        break;
    case 3:
        cout << "x is 3";
        break;
    default:
        cout << "x is not 1, 2, or 3";
}
```

In this code, x is 2, so the case where x is 2 will be executed. With the break statement after the cout statement, once that case is executed, the program will jump out of the switch-case structure. Without the break, it would continue to check case 3 and the default case. 

Remember that using a break statement should be handled with care because it can sometimes lead to logic error. For example, if a condition to break a loop is rarely met, it might cause an infinite loop.

