Based on your query, you are looking to swap variables of same type within a tuple without using a temporary variable or the STL swap function. This is often done using arithmetic swapping or XOR swap algorithm for integer types. Now, in C++, tuples are immutable, which means the value of an element can't be changed once it's set. Hence, swapping like how we do it in regular variables can't be done.

A possible way to achieve an effect similar to swapping would be to create a new tuple with the elements in your desired order.

However, let's come up with a bit of a workaround and assume that we have a custom mutable tuple and we are dealing with integer types. We could achieve a swap without temp variable using arithmetic like so:

```cpp
void swap(int &a, int &b) {
    a = a + b;
    b = a - b;
    a = a - b;
}
```

Or using XOR swap:

```cpp
void swap(int &a, int &b) {
    if (&a != &b) {
        a ^= b;
        b ^= a;
        a ^= b;
    }
}
```

However, please note that arithmetic swapping and XOR swap can cause overflow issues and are typically considered bad practice. They are also affected by the "strict aliasing rule", which might lead your code to produce not well-defined results, including but not limited to misbehaving or crashing.

In practice, using a temporary variable or STL swap are the best solutions to avoid these issues:

```cpp
void swap(int &a, int &b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
```

So, while theoretically possible to avoid a temp variable or STL swap, it's typically best not to do this unless you have a very good reason and understand all these risks and limitations completely.

