In C++, you can reverse a C-style string by swapping characters from both ends and then moving towards the center.

To handle Unicode characters correctly, you can't simply reverse the bytes, because one Unicode character may span more than one byte. A robust solution is to use a library such as ICU which provides utilities for correctly manipulating Unicode strings.

However, you asked not to use any inbuilt function, and handling Unicode String without a Library would be too complex to be reasonable.

So, here's a simple C++ code for reversing a C-style string that correctly handles ASCII characters (not Unicode).

As for preventing buffer overruns, make sure that the provided string is correctly null-terminated and that we do not go beyond the bounds of the array.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
#include<cassert>
using namespace std;

void reverseString(char* str) {
    assert(str != NULL);

    // Get the length of the string
    char* end = str;
    while (*end) ++end;
    --end;

    // Swap characters from both ends
    while(str < end){
        char temp = *str;
        *str = *end;
        *end = temp;

        str++;
        end--;
    }
}

int main() {
  char str[] = "Hello World!";
  reverseString(str);
  cout << str << endl; // Outputs: "!dlroW olleH"

  return 0;
}
