To implement a data structure that supports the mentioned operations efficiently, we can use a combination of a doubly linked list and a hash map. The doubly linked list will allow us to efficiently add and remove elements from the front, while the hash map will allow us to access elements in constant time.

Here's the Python implementation of the data structure:

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
        self.prev = None
        self.next = None

class EfficientDataStructure:
    def __init__(self):
        self.head = None
        self.tail = None
        self.node_map = {}

    def push_front(self, t):
        new_node = Node(t)
        if not self.head:
            self.head = new_node
            self.tail = new_node
        else:
            new_node.next = self.head
            self.head.prev = new_node
            self.head = new_node
        self.node_map[t] = new_node

    def pop_front(self):
        if not self.head:
            return None
        value = self.head.value
        if self.head == self.tail:
            self.head = None
            self.tail = None
        else:
            self.head = self.head.next
            self.head.prev = None
        del self.node_map[value]
        return value

    def peek_front(self):
        if self.head:
            return self.head.value
        return None
```

In this implementation, the `push_front` operation adds a new node to the front of the doubly linked list and updates the hash map with the new node. The `pop_front` operation removes the node from the front of the list and updates the hash map accordingly. The `peek_front` operation simply returns the value of the node at the front of the list.

This data structure allows all operations to be performed in constant time complexity, O(1), as required.

