Yes, Boost.Asio library is providing a model for dealing with asynchronous I/O which is similar to IO Completion Ports (IOCP).

Boost.Asio provides an abstraction over this model, providing asynchronous read/write operations, managing IO services and thread pools for you. Additionally, Boost.Asio is portable across different platforms and include support for other I/O operations such as timers and signals.

Here's a high level example of how you might use Boost.Asio:

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <boost/bind.hpp>
#include <boost/thread.hpp>

// The io_service represents your application's link to the operating system's I/O services.
boost::asio::io_service io_service;

// A work object represents some work that needs to be done. 
// The constructor for work will inform the io_service that there is work to do. 
// The io_service.run() function will not exit while there is work to do.
boost::asio::io_service::work work(io_service);

// Start a number of threads to service the I/O operations. 
// In this example we'll start 4 threads in a thread group.
boost::thread_group threads;
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
    threads.create_thread(boost::bind(&boost::asio::io_service::run, &io_service));

// Post some work to the io_service. post() will cause the provided handler to be called, 
// but without allowing the io_service::run() call to exit if there is no more work left to do.

for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
    io_service.post(boost::bind(worker_function, i));

// Wait for all threads in the group to exit.
threads.join_all();
```

Here you can see an asio service is created to represent your IO operations and a pool of threads are created to service these operations in parallel.

