In the below code, we will first create a very simple recursive function "computeFactorial" to calculate the factorial of any number. Then we will create a memoization function "FactorialMemoization" to avoid redundant calculations. We'll use a map to save the values. In addition, we'll handle edge cases, such as negative numbers and non-integer inputs by controlling the input with conditions. Here is a simple representation in C++.

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

// Recursive function to compute factorial
int computeFactorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
        return 1;
    else
        return n * computeFactorial(n - 1);
}

// Usage of map for memorization to avoid redundancy
map<int,int> memo;
int FactorialMemoization(int n) {
    if (memo.count(n)) return memo[n];
    if (n == 0||n==1) {
      memo[n] = 1;
    } else {
      memo[n] = n * FactorialMemoization(n-1);
    }
    return memo[n];
}

int main() {
    cout << "Enter a positive integer: ";
    double n; cin >> n;

    // Checking for non-integer inputs and handle errors
    if (n != (int)n) {
        cout << "Input is not an integer!";
        return -1;
    }

    // Checking for negative numbers and handle errors.
    if (n < 0) {
        cout << "Factorial of negative numbers is not defined!";
        return -1;
    }

    // Checking for number beyond 20 and handle errors.
    if (n > 20) {
        cout << "Input shouldn't exceed 20!";
        return -1;
    }

    // Compute factorial with memoization
    int result = FactorialMemoization((int)n);
    cout << "The factorial is: " << result << endl;  
}
```

This program will calculate the factorial of a number and intelligently handle negative or invalid inputs. However, C++ does not have built-in ways to avoid stack overflow in recursion if the number is too large. We can handle it using dynamic programming. Also, standard C++ could overflow the int datatype if the input is larger than 20!.

