In C++, multiple inheritance occurs when a class can inherit from more than one parent class. This can lead to a problem called the "diamond problem" when the same class is inherited through different paths causing ambiguity. The diamond problem can be resolved by using virtual inheritance.

Let's consider an example. Assumed we have a class Animal which is inherited by the classes Mammal and Bird. Then, another class, Bat, is inherited from both Mammal and Bird. If there are some methods with the same name in both Mammal and Bird, there would be confusion to which method to use in the Bat class. 

To solve this problem, we can use virtual inheritance. This is shown in the code below:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal {
public:
    int age;
    void furColor() {
        cout << "The fur color is not known.\n";
    }
};


class Mammal: virtual public Animal {
public:
    void furColor() {
        cout << "The mammal's fur color is brown.\n";
    }
};

class Bird: virtual public Animal {
public:
    void furColor() {
        cout << "The bird's fur color is white.\n";
    }
};


class Bat: public Mammal, public Bird {
public:
    void furColor() {
        cout << "The bat's fur color is black.\n";
    }
};


int main() {
    Bat b;
    b.furColor();
    return 0;
}
```
This program defines a class Animal with a method furColor(). Mammal and Bird inherit from Animal and override furColor(). Bat inherits from both Mammal and Bird in such a way that there is no ambiguity in the method to choose when furColor is invoked.

The choice of using virtual inheritance here is to ensure that only one copy of Animal is inherited by Bat, thereby eliminating the potential ambiguity in calling same-named methods.

This way, when we call "b.furColor()" in the main function, it does not matter that both Mammal and Bird have a method named furColor(), since Bat has its own version of furColor(). This is a way to handle scenarios where multiple base classes have methods with the same name.

This solution is created for handling the base classes that have methods with the same name, as well as to avoid the diamond problem through virtual inheritance in C++. If there's any other special scenario or requirement, I would adjust or refactor my design approach accordingly.

Here is the corrected version of reversing a doubly linked list using C++:

```C++
class Node {
    public: 
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;
};

// Function to reverse a Doubly Linked List in C++
void reverse(Node** head_ref) {
    Node* temp = NULL;
    Node* current = *head_ref;
    while (current != NULL) {
        temp = current->prev;
        current->prev = current->next;
        current->next = temp;
        current = current->prev;
    }

    if(temp != NULL )
        *head_ref = temp->prev;
}
```

The time complexity of the above implementation is O(n) because it traverses the linked list only once. Here 'n' is the quantity of nodes in the doubly linked list.

This time complexity could be further optimized if we could reverse the list in half the time i.e., in O(n/2). However, due to the sequential access nature of Linked Lists, it is not possible to access the middle element in O(1) time. Thus, we stick to O(n) time complexity which is the best we can get for reversing a doubly linked list.

In terms of space, the algorithm behaves quite well as it only uses a fixed amount of space and thus has O(1) space complexity. As the whole list reversal happens in place, we are not using any additional space that grows with the input size or number of nodes in the list.

For large linked lists, this solution would work efficiently both in terms of time and space. Even with considerably large volume of data, this would only moderately impact the performance – proportionally to the size of the linked list. However, there won't be exponential increment in time taken with the size of the linked list, which is a favorable outcome.

