2. To find time complexity: 

   Merge sort algorithm divides the array into two halves, sorts them recursively, and then merge these sorted halves. The time complexity of this process is O(n log n) irrespective of the order of the elements in the array, because even if an array is reversed, you still need to divide and sort the array till the smallest unit, which gives you the O(n log n) time complexity.

"n" is the number of elements and "log n" is the number of splits that will be made by the merge sort algorithm.

So even if you reverse your array before applying merge sort, its time complexity still remains O(n log n). 

This is because Merge Sort is not affected by the original ordering of the array—it will still perform the same amount of comparisons and operations. This is in contrast to other sorting algorithms like Bubble Sort or Insertion Sort which might have a best case performance of O(n) if the array is already sorted. 

As a result, Merge Sort is a great choice if the input size (the number of elements in the array) is large, even if the array is sorted, reversed, or randomly arranged.

