Creating a full DOM tree when creating large XML files is not a great idea as it significantly consumes memory. Here is a suggestion of writing XML in a streaming manner, using the XML writer library provided by libxml2, which is ANSI C compliant.

```cpp
#include <libxml/xmlwriter.h>

int main() {
    xmlTextWriterPtr writer;
    int rc;

    /* Create a new XmlWriter for uri, with no compression. */
    writer = xmlNewTextWriterFilename("test.xml", 0);
    if (writer == NULL) {
        printf("TestXmlwriterFilename: Error creating the xml writer");
        return -1;
    }

    /* Start an element named "EXAMPLE". */
    rc = xmlTextWriterStartElement(writer, BAD_CAST "EXAMPLE");
    if (rc < 0) {
        printf("TestXmlwriterFilename: Error at xmlTextWriterStartElement\n");
        return -1;
    }

    /* Add an attribute with name "Attribute" and value "yes" to the element. */
    rc = xmlTextWriterWriteAttribute(writer, BAD_CAST "Attribute", BAD_CAST "yes");
    if (rc < 0) {
        printf("TestXmlwriterFilename: Error at xmlTextWriterWriteAttribute\n");
        return -1;
    }

    /* Close the element named EXAMPLE. */
    rc = xmlTextWriterEndElement(writer);
    if (rc < 0) {
        printf("TestXmlwriterFilename: Error at xmlTextWriterEndElement\n");
        return -1;
    }

    xmlFreeTextWriter(writer);

    return 0;
}
```

This is simply a build-up from top-level node to leaf-node information. Once a node with its attributes is finished writing, it will release the memory immediately, making it possible to handle large XML files.

If you want to handle escaping characters, libxml2 also provides the method `xmlTextWriterWriteElement` that can handle escaping in a standard mechanism. 

Note: libxml2 might not be a built-in library in your system, and you might need to download it. It's a mature open-source library and is widely used, even in some XML utilities in Linux system.

