In real-world software development, memory leaks could severely slow down the system performance, or even cause the system to crash in severe cases. This usually occurs in long-running programs (like servers) where small leaks could buildup over time and use up a significant chunk of memory. 

Preventing memory leaks usually involves good coding practices and rigorous testing:
1. Avoid allocating memory dynamically unless necessary.
2. Always deallocate memory after you're done using it. Pair every 'new' with 'delete' in C++.
3. Use smart pointers (like unique_ptr or shared_ptr in C++) which can automatically deallocate memory when no longer in use. 
4. Use memory profiling and debugging tools to detect leaks.
5. Extensive system and performance testing to catch memory leaks before going to production.

