You are working on a financial modeling application that involves simulating asset returns using a multivariate normal distribution. To achieve this, you need to generate random samples from a multivariate normal distribution given the mean vector and the covariance matrix. You decide to use the Cholesky decomposition method to achieve this.

Write a C++ function that takes the mean vector, the covariance matrix, and the number of samples as input and returns a matrix of random samples from the multivariate normal distribution.

The Cholesky decomposition method involves the following steps:
1. Compute the Cholesky decomposition of the covariance matrix.
2. Generate random samples from a standard normal distribution.
3. Multiply the Cholesky decomposition matrix by the random samples and add the mean vector to obtain the final samples.

You can use the Eigen library for matrix operations and random number generation.

Your task is to implement the function `generateMultivariateNormalSamples` that takes the mean vector, the covariance matrix, and the number of samples as input and returns a matrix of random samples from the multivariate normal distribution.

```cpp
#include <Eigen/Dense>
#include <random>

Eigen::MatrixXd generateMultivariateNormalSamples(const Eigen::VectorXd& mean, const Eigen::MatrixXd& covariance, int numSamples) {
    Eigen::LLT<Eigen::MatrixXd> lltOfCov(covariance);
    Eigen::MatrixXd samples(mean.size(), numSamples);

    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::normal_distribution<> normalDist(0, 1);

    for (int i = 0; i < numSamples; i++) {
        Eigen::VectorXd z(mean.size());
        for (int j = 0; j < mean.size(); j++)
            z(j) = normalDist(gen);

        samples.col(i) = mean + lltOfCov.matrixL() * z;
    }

    return samples;
}
