3. `if (n < 2) {return false;}`: If the input number is less than 2, then immediately return false because no numbers less than 2 are considered prime.

4. `else if (n == 2) {return true;}`: If the input number is 2, then immediately return true because 2 is the smallest prime number.

5. `for (int i = 2; i <= std::sqrt(n); ++i)`: Iterate over values from 2 to the square root of n. We only need to check up till the square root of n because a larger factor of n must be a multiple of smaller factor that has already been checked.

6. `if (n % i == 0) {return false;}`: If n is evenly divisible by i (i.e., the remainder when n is divided by i is 0), then n is not a prime number so return false.

