However, in the for loop, the condition stated the loop will run from 0 to 10 (inclusive). But since there is no index 10 in 'arr', referencing 'arr[10]' exceeds the bound of the array. It will access memory that is outside the assigned space for the array.

This is called an 'off-by-one' error and it is a common mistake in programming. It can lead to unpredictable behavior, as it may modify data in the memory location that happens to be next to your array. In some cases, it can cause a crash if it ends up accessing a restricted memory space. 

Here is the corrected version of the loop:
```C++
for (int i=0; i<10; i++) {
    arr[i] = i*i;
}
```
This loop will iterate over indices 0 through 9, which are all valid indices for the array 'arr'.

