Here is an example of a multi-threaded C++ Fibonacci sequence program that allows the user to specify an integer divisor that will filter out Fibonacci sequence numbers. Multiple threads are used and synchronization is achieved through the use of locks.

It should be mentioned that c++ doesn't come with a built in thread functionality (before C++11), So, You'll need to have a C++11 or newer version. Also your output will not always be the same because thread execution order handled by operation system, not by the code.

The sample program receives an input `n` from the user and only prints those Fibonacci numbers that are divisible by `n`. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx;

int fibonacci(int n) 
{
    int f[n + 2];
    f[0] = 0;
    f[1] = 1;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      f[i] = f[i - 1] + f[i - 2];
    }

    return f[n];
}

void print_fibonacci(int n, int divisor) 
{ 
    for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        int fib = fibonacci(i);
        if(fib % divisor == 0) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            std::cout << fib << " ";
        }
    }
}

int main() 
{
    int n, divisor;
    std::cout << "Enter the range for Fibonacci sequence: ";
    std::cin >> n;
    std::cout << "Enter the divisor: ";
    std::cin >> divisor;

    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for(int i = 0; i < std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); i++) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(print_fibonacci, n, divisor));
    }

    for(auto& th : threads) {
        th.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program first asks the user for an integer to use as a divisor in determining which Fibonacci numbers to print. It then creates threads to compute the Fibonacci sequence numbers in parallel, and then print the numbers out, using a lock to ensure that the threads do not interfere with each other's output. Note: the complexity of the fibonacci sequence in the program is high, this code may run slowly for larger inputs because of this.

