Here is an example demonstrating implicit pointer conversion, polymorphism and memory management in multi-threaded environment: 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

// Base class
class Base {
public:
    virtual void show() { std::cout << "Base\n"; }
};

// Derived class
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void show() override { std::cout << "Derived\n"; }
};

// Function to play with polymorphism
void playWithPolymorphism(Base* b, std::mutex &m) {
    // Ensuring thread-safe write to stdout
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(m);
    b->show();
}

int main() {
    std::mutex m; // Used for synchronizing output

    // Using unique_ptr for automatic and exception-safe memory management
    std::unique_ptr<Base> b1(new Base);
    std::unique_ptr<Derived> d1(new Derived);

    // Threads
    std::thread t1(playWithPolymorphism, b1.get(), std::ref(m));
    std::thread t2(playWithPolymorphism, d1.get(), std::ref(m));

    // Joining threads
    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, Base is the base class, and Derived is a class derived from Base. We create two unique pointers for Base and Derived, and two threads, each calls the function "playWithPolymorphism" with unique pointers passed to this function. The mutex is passed to ensure proper synchronization when printing to the stdout. In C++, implicit pointer conversion will convert the Derived class pointer to Base class pointer when necessary.

As the function playWithPolymorphism accepts a pointer to Base class as an argument, our threads t1 and t2 both call playWithPolymorphism with b1 and d1 which are unique pointer objects holding the pointers towards Base and Derived class instances.

The unique_ptr in C++ aids in safe memory handling - it is a kind of smart pointer that destroys the object it points to as soon as the unique_ptr goes out of scope.

