What would be the most efficient way to compare two `double` or two `float` values?

Simply doing this is not correct:


bool CompareDoubles1 (double A, double B)
{
   return A == B;
}



But something like:


bool CompareDoubles2 (double A, double B) 
{
   diff = A - B;
   return (diff < EPSILON) && (-diff < EPSILON);
}



Seems to waste processing.

Does anyone know a smarter float comparer?

a:
It is generally a good idea to compare floating point numbers using some tolerance (defined by EPSILON in your code). Another way to write this which is slightly more efficient and it might be simpler to understand is:

```cpp
bool CompareDoubles(double A, double B) 
{
   return fabs(A - B) < EPSILON;
}
```

This calculates the absolute difference between A and B and checks if it is less than a small constant (EPSILON). This means that A and B are considered equal if they are "close enough," which bypasses many of the issues with floating point precision.

Remember to define EPSILON regarding the precision you need.

