In C++, a namespace is a declarative region that provides a scope. It is a way of segmenting the global scope into multiple smaller scopes, allowing coders to group named entities that otherwise would have global scope into narrower scopes. The main purpose of a namespace is to prevent name conflicts in large projects. Symbols declared inside a namespace won't conflict with identical names declared in another namespace or the global scope.

Consider the example of two libraries: 'LibraryA' and 'LibraryB'. Both libraries have a function with the same name, say 'print()'.

LibraryA.h:
```c++
namespace LibraryA {
    void print() { // Print something }
}
```

LibraryB.h:
```c++
namespace LibraryB {
  void print() { // Print something else }
}
```

Here, both the libraries have a function with the same name 'print'. Normally, if you include both these headers in a source file, you would get a name collision issue because C++ wouldn't know which 'print' method you are referring to when you call it.

But, with namespaces, we've declared each 'print' within a separate namespace, which avoids this issue.

Consider you have included both libraries into 'main.cpp'

```c++
#include "LibraryA.h"
#include "LibraryB.h"

int main() {
  LibraryA::print(); // Calls print function of LibraryA
  LibraryB::print(); // Calls print function of LibraryB
  return 0;
}
```
Here we're gating each call to 'print' with the appropriate namespace.

If you want to avoid writing the entire namespace name each time, you can use namespace aliasing. 

```c++
namespace LA = LibraryA;

int main() {
 LA::print();  // Referring LibraryA's print function using alias
 LibraryB::print();
 return 0;
}
```
Here LA is an alias for LibraryA.

You can also use the 'using' directive to bring the entire namespace into your current scope or just a single function.

```c++
using namespace LibraryA;

int main() {
  print();  // This will call LibraryA's print as it is brought in scope.
  LibraryB::print();
  return 0;
}
```
In this case, 'print' refers to LibraryA's print because we have declared 'using namespace LibraryA'. 

Please note, bringing an entire namespace into the scope is generally not recommended because it may cause name conflicts with other parts of the code which is not under your control. It's always better to bring in only what's needed using 'using LibraryA::print;' (this brings only print function into the scope) or use namespace aliasing or namespace specific calling.

