To solve this problem, we can use a two-pointer approach to reverse the string in-place. We will start with one pointer at the beginning of the string and another at the end, then swap the characters at these positions and move the pointers towards each other until they meet in the middle.

Here's the implementation of the `reverseString` function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

namespace cudf::test {
    std::string reverseString(const std::string& input) {
        std::string reversed = input;  // Create a copy of the input string
        int left = 0;  // Pointer to the beginning of the string
        int right = reversed.length() - 1;  // Pointer to the end of the string

        while (left < right) {
            // Swap characters at the left and right pointers
            char temp = reversed[left];
            reversed[left] = reversed[right];
            reversed[right] = temp;

            // Move the pointers towards each other
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return reversed;  // Return the reversed string
    }
}  // namespace cudf::test
```

This implementation efficiently reverses the input string without using any built-in functions or libraries for string manipulation.

