In C++, we don't have inbuilt heap data structure but priority_queue can do the sorting part. Also, we don't have list comprehension in C++ either. Here we go:

```c++
#include<queue>
#include<vector>

std::vector<int> strange_sort_list_bst(std::vector<int> lst) {
    // Use priority_queue for efficient minimal and maximal value extraction
    // Make maximum heap for negative numbers
    std::priority_queue<int> max_heap;
    // Make minimum heap for non-negative numbers
    std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> min_heap;

    for (int number : lst) {
        if (number < 0) {
            max_heap.push(-number);
        } else {
            min_heap.push(number);
        }
    }

    // Initialize result vector
    std::vector<int> res;

    // Alter between extracting minimal and maximal value
    while (!min_heap.empty() || !max_heap.empty()) {
        // First extract minimal value
        if (!min_heap.empty()) {
            res.push_back(min_heap.top());
            min_heap.pop();
        }
        // Then extract maximal value
        if (!max_heap.empty()) {
            res.push_back(-max_heap.top());
            max_heap.pop();
        }
    }

    return res;
}
```

In C++, we use std::vector for dynamic arrays instead of Python's list. The negative numbers are stored as positives in max_heap and when we are to use them, we change the sign. pop() and push() methods in C++ doesn't return the value popped, so we have to use top() method to access the topmost element. Also in C++, we check if a container is empty by its empty() method.

