#include <vector>

std::vector<int> fib(50, 0);

void generate(int n) {
    for (int i = 2; i < n; ++i) {
        fib[i] = fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2];
    }
}

void print_fib(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cout << fib[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(generate, 50);
    std::thread t2(print_fib, 50);
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    return 0;
}
```
In the given program, the two threads may not execute in the desired order which could result in a race condition and incorrect output. Make sure to take this into account and adjust accordingly in your own implementation.

a:
Race condition occurs when two or more threads can access shared data and they try to change it at the same time. As a result, values of variables may be unpredictable and vary depending on the timings of context switches of the operating system. A simple way to avoid racing condition is to use synchronization mechanisms such as mutexes or locks.

Here is the modified C++ code which ensures that the threads are synchronized and the output is printed correctly.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> fib(50, 0);
std::mutex mtx; 

void generate(int n) {
    fib[0] = 0;
    fib[1] = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i < n; ++i) {
        fib[i] = fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2];
    }
}

void print_fib(int n) {
    mtx.lock();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cout << fib[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    mtx.unlock();    
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(generate, 50);
    t1.join();

    std::thread t2(print_fib, 50);
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, `std::thread t1(generate, 50);` creates a new thread and executes `generate(50)` on it and similarly for `print_fib(50)`. `t1.join()` will make the main thread wait for `t1` to finish execution before moving on to creating `t2`. This ensures correct order of execution.

`std::mutex mtx;` defines a mutex. In `print_fib(int)`, `mtx.lock();` locks the mutex stopping other threads from entering the critical section, and `mtx.unlock();` unlocks it allowing other threads to enter. This ensures that the printing task is thread-safe.

Do note that the choice of datatype `int` limits the result to 47 numbers only, as 48th fibonacci number exceeds the maximum limit of an `int`. You can use `long long int` or `__int128` for larger numbers.

