Benefits of using AOP alongside OOP:

1. Modularity: Using Aspect-Oriented Programming enhances modularity by capturing and modularizing cross-cutting concerns separately from the main responsibilities of a class, interfaces, or methods. 

2. Easy Maintenance: It is easier to add, change or remove cross-cutting concerns of the application with AOP because it is well separated from the business logic. 

3. Enhancing Readability: It improves readability and understandability of the program as the business logic is not littered by system services' codes. 

4. Scalability: AOP scales well for large software systems because you can just add new aspects, or modify existing ones where needed, without having to touch the class behavior or the business logic itself. 

Constraints & Potential Pitfalls of using AOP in C++:

1. Not Natively Supported: Unlike in some other languages such as Java, C++ does not natively support AOP. You'd have to use libraries such as AspectC++ to add support for aspect-oriented programming. 

2. Learning Curve: Aspect-oriented programming introduces an additional paradigm on top of object-oriented programming. Developers accustomed to OOP needs sufficient time to learn and adopt the concepts in AOP. 

3. Debugging: Debugging can be challenging in AOP due to code scattering and tangling causing the flow of the program to be non-linear.

C++ doesn't natively support AOP, AspectC++ is a language that extends C++ with aspect-oriented features. An erroneous AspectC++ code would look like this:

```cpp
aspect ErroneousAspect {
  advice call("String Logger::%%(...)"): before() {
    cout << "Entering function " << JoinPoint::signature() << "\n";
  }
}
```
The erroneous part in the above code is "String Logger::%%(...)". This is invalid in AspectC++.

Here is the rectified version:

```cpp
aspect LoggingAspect {
  advice call("% Logger::%(...)"): before() {
    cout << "Entering function " << JoinPoint::signature() << "\n";
  }
}
```
The advice part is corrected to "Logger::%(...)". This advice will apply before every method call on Logger instances, printing out the name and parameters of the method being called. 

The use of aspect-oriented programming in this way modularizes the logging aspect of the system, which enhances maintainability since changes to this concern only have to be made in one place. It also improves scalability since adding logging to new method calls is just a matter of modifying the pointcut expression.

