Yes, a simple way to make a STL map persistent would be to write it to a file in a serialized form and later read it back. Here's an example using a std::map<int, std::string>:

We'll use two helper functions: one for serialization (`write`) and one for deserialization (`read`).

```cpp
#include <map>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void write(const std::map<int,std::string>& map)
{
    std::ofstream file("map.txt");
    for(auto const &pair: map)
    {
        file <<pair.first <<'\n'<< pair.second<<'\n';
    }
}

std::map<int,std::string> read()
{
    std::ifstream file("map.txt");
    std::map<int,std::string> map;
    while(file)
    {
        int key;
        file >> key;
        std::string str;
        std::getline(file >> std::ws, str);
        if(!str.empty())
            map[key] = str;
    }
    return map;
}

int main()
{
    std::map<int,std::string> map={ {1,"str1"}, {2,"str2"} };
    write(map);

    std::map<int,std::string> newMap=read();
    for(auto const &pair: newMap)
    {
        std::cout<<"key: "<<pair.first<<" value: "<<pair.second<<'\n';
    }
}
```

This simple serialization method assumes that the data inside the map is simple (i.e., can be directly written / read from a stream operator). If your map contains complex objects, you might need to use some kind of serialization library (like Boost.Serialization, cereal, or others).

Also please remember that this way of serialization can be fragile. Changes in type of key/value would probably break it, or white spaces in std::string (as it uses them to recognize when one entry ends and another starts), and so on. If your objects/values are complex, you would need a more sophisticated solution, like a serialization library that you could find in Boost/Cereal or some handcrafted method better fitted for your purposes.

