    IMG_AddInstrumentFunction(Image, 0);

    PIN_StartProgram();
    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, the pintool hooks into `malloc` calls and prints the size of the requested allocation before `malloc` is actually called.

However, keep in mind that this will not give you the heap allocations/deallocations done by libc or other system libraries which might not use `malloc`, `calloc`, etc. In such cases, you would need to hook into the relevant system calls (like `mmap`, `mprotect`, `brk`, `sbrk`, etc.) directly.

An alternative approach might be to combine Pin with other tools, such as using Pin to inject code into the target program that would call an external tool (like `/proc/pid/maps` for Linux systems) and get the required heap information. 

