There's a functionality built into the Windows API known as file and folder change notifications, which invokes a callback whenever specified events occur. 

In C++, you use the `ReadDirectoryChangesW()` function to set up this callback (use the WINAPI version). 

Below is a simple example on how you could set up the function:

```cpp
    HANDLE hDir = CreateFile(path_to_watch, FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY, 
                             FILE_SHARE_READ | FILE_SHARE_WRITE | FILE_SHARE_DELETE,
                             NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS, NULL);

    const DWORD buffer_length = 1024;
    char buffer[buffer_length] = {0};
    DWORD bytes_returned = 0;

    if (ReadDirectoryChangesW(hDir, buffer, buffer_length, FALSE, 
                              FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_LAST_WRITE, &bytes_returned, NULL, NULL))
    {
        printf("Change detected\n");
    }
```

The code first obtains a handle to the directory in question using `CreateFile()`, and then sets up the callback using `ReadDirectoryChangesW()` to notify us if there are any modifications (`FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_LAST_WRITE`) to files in the directory. 

You should place this in a loop to keep listening for changes. When a change is detected, bytes_returned will be set to the number of bytes returned in the buffer, which contains information about what changed. 

Note, however, that using directory change notifications in this way is generally, best for apps that run all the time monitoring the file system, like sync clients or antivirus programs. 

If you only occasionally need to check if a file's changed since the last time you checked then you’re better off just scanning the directory when needed, which is a lot simpler and safer.

And No, there is no registry entry to modify this behavior. The time stamp on the directory is not meant to be an indicator of when any files in the directory were last updated.

