int receiveTextSegment(const SOCKET *receive_socket, char *receiveText, int size, bool show) {
    if (size >= MAX_TEXT - 1)
        size = MAX_TEXT - 1; // Truncate size if it exceeds the maximum text length

    while (size > 0) {
        int receive_size = minOf(BUFFER_SIZE, size); // Determine the size to receive
        int received_bytes = recv(*receive_socket, receiveText, receive_size, 0); // Receive data from the socket
        if (received_bytes <= 0) {
            // Handle receive error or connection closed
            return -1;
        }
        receiveText += received_bytes; // Move the receiveText pointer forward
        size -= received_bytes; // Decrease the remaining size to receive
    }

    receiveText[MAX_TEXT - 1] = '\0'; // Ensure null-termination within the maximum text length

    if (show) {
        printf("%s\n", receiveText); // Display the received text if show is true
    }

    return 0; // Return 0 to indicate successful reception and processing
}
```

In the solution, the `receiveTextSegment` function first truncates the `size` if it exceeds the maximum text length. Then, it enters a loop to receive data from the socket in chunks of size determined by `minOf(BUFFER_SIZE, size)`. The function handles potential receive errors or connection closure and ensures null-termination within the maximum text length. Finally, if `show` is true, the received text is displayed. The function returns 0 to indicate successful reception and processing.

