Explanation:

In C++, there are two ways to pass arguments to functions: call by value and call by reference.

1. In call by value, the function creates a new copy of the argument passed and performs operations on it. Any changes made are not affected on the original argument.

2. In call by reference, instead of taking the values of the argument and making a copy of it, the function operates on the reference directly; hence, any changes made to the argument are reflected on the original variable itself.

The following C++ program demonstrates this difference.

```C++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

// function to demonstrate call by value
void callByValue(int num) {
	num = num * 2; // attempt to modify the value
	cout << "Inside callByValue function, num = " << num << '\n';
}

// function to demonstrate call by reference
void callByReference(int& num) {
	num = num * 2; // attempt to modify the reference
	cout << "Inside callByReference function, num = " << num << '\n';
}

int main() {
	int a = 5;

	cout << "Initial value of a = " << a << '\n';

	// calling the function with call by value
	callByValue(a);
	cout << "After callByValue function, a = " << a << '\n'; // value of a doesn't change as in call by value, it makes a new copy of the argument

	// calling the function with call by reference
	callByReference(a);
	cout << "After callByReference function, a = " << a << '\n'; // value of a changes as in call by reference, the function operates on the reference directly

	return 0;
}
```

In this program, when we use call by value, the value of 'a' does not change after the function call because the function just modifies a copy of 'a', not 'a' itself. But when we use call by reference, any changes the function makes to 'a' is reflected in the original 'a', so the value of 'a' is changed after the function call.

