```cpp
int KthSmallestElement(Node *root, int &K)
{
    if (root == NULL)
        return -1;

    // Stack to simulate the recursive in-order traversal
    stack<Node *> s;
    Node *curr = root;

    while (curr != NULL || !s.empty())
    {
        while (curr != NULL)
        {
            s.push(curr);
            curr = curr->left;
        }

        curr = s.top();
        s.pop();

        // Decrement K for each visited node
        K--;
        if (K == 0)
            return curr->data;

        curr = curr->right;
    }

    return -1; // K is out of range
}
```

The solution uses a stack to simulate the in-order traversal of the BST. It iterates through the BST until it finds the Kth smallest element, decrementing K for each visited node. If K becomes 0, the function returns the current node's data as the Kth smallest element. If K is out of range or the BST is empty, the function returns -1.

