Here is an outline of the C++ function which calculates the standard deviation of an STL vector:

1. Include necessary libraries. You will need `vector`, `cmath`, and `numeric` C++ Standard Libraries.
2. Define the function `calculateSD()` which accepts a vector of integers.
3. Calculate the mean (average) of the vector elements using `std::accumulate()` function from the `numeric` library, then divide it by the size of the vector.
4. Next, calculate the variance of the vector elements. It is defined as the average of the squares of deviations of values from mean.
5. For each value in the vector, subtract mean and square the result (the squared deviation).
6. Calculate the average of those squared deviations (variance).
7. Finally, use `std::sqrt()` function from the `cmath` library to get the square root of the variance, which is standard deviation.

Here is the code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <numeric>

double calculateSD(std::vector<int> data) {
    double sum = std::accumulate(data.begin(), data.end(), 0.0);
    double mean = sum / data.size();

    double sq_sum = std::inner_product(data.begin(), data.end(), data.begin(), 0.0);
    double stdev = std::sqrt(sq_sum / data.size() - mean * mean);

    return stdev;
}
```

You can call this function with your vector to get its standard deviation:

