Fibonacci sequence is defined as each number is the sum of two preceding ones, starting from 0 and 1. So the code below will generate this sequence.


```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

// Function to generate Fibonacci sequence
std::vector<int> generate_fibonacci(int limit) {
    std::vector<int> fibonacci;
    fibonacci.push_back(0);
    fibonacci.push_back(1);

    for (int i = 2; ; ++i) {
        int next = fibonacci[i - 1] + fibonacci[i - 2];
        if (next > limit) break;
        fibonacci.push_back(next);
    }

    return fibonacci;
}

// Function to check if a number belongs to Fibonacci sequence
bool is_fibonacci(std::vector<int> &fibonacci, int num) {
    // Use binary_search since the Fibonacci sequence is sorted
    return std::binary_search(fibonacci.begin(), fibonacci.end(), num);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> fibonacci = generate_fibonacci(1000);

    for (int i = 0; i < fibonacci.size(); ++i) {
        std::cout << fibonacci[i] << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    int num = 21;
    if (is_fibonacci(fibonacci, num)) {
        std::cout << num << " belongs to the Fibonacci sequence." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << num << " does not belong to the Fibonacci sequence." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a vector to store the Fibonacci sequence, then it uses a simple loop to generate the sequence. The `break` statement is used to exit the loop once the limit has been reached, to avoid storing unnecessary numbers and to keep time complexity in check.

To check whether a particular number is in the Fibonacci sequence, the program uses the `binary_search` function from the `<algorithm>` library, which performs a binary search on a sorted range of data. This is more efficient than a linear search (which would have a time complexity of O(n)), because a binary search has an average time complexity of O(log n).

This code has a space complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the Fibonacci sequence that are less than or equal to the upper limit (1000 in this case). This is because it stores the entire sequence in memory. The time complexity is also O(n) for generating the sequence plus O(log n) for the binary search, which is quite efficient.

