In C++, we don't have the same built-in functional programming constructs and Optional types like in Java. So the equivalent code will look quite different:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <optional>

class Solution {
public:
    std::vector<std::optional<int>> largestSmallestIntegers(std::vector<int> lst) {
        std::optional<int> maxNeg;
        std::optional<int> minPos;

        for(int i : lst) {
            if(i < 0 && (!maxNeg.has_value() || i > *maxNeg))
                maxNeg = i;
            if(i > 0 && (!minPos.has_value() || i < *minPos))
                minPos = i;
        }

        return {maxNeg, minPos};
    }
};
```

In this code, `std::optional` is used to represent an optional value (a value that can be absent). Please note the use of `.has_value()` to check whether the `std::optional` contains a value, and the `*` operator to access the contained value. If the `std::optional` does not have a value and you try to access it with the `*` operator, the behavior is undefined. Hence, always check with `.has_value()` before accessing the value.

