The C++ code below fulfills the specification by checking for each number less than or equal to n whether it's prime, and if it is, adds it to the sum variable.

```C++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

bool is_prime(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return false;
    if (n <= 3) return true;

    if (n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0)
        return false;

    for (long long i = 5; i * i <= n; i += 6)
        if (n % i == 0 || n % (i + 2) == 0)
            return false;

    return true;
}

int sum_to_n(int n){
    int sum = 0;
    for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++){
        if (is_prime(i)) {
            sum += i;
        }
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    cout << sum_to_n(30) << endl; // 129
    cout << sum_to_n(10) << endl; // 17
    cout << sum_to_n(5) << endl; // 10
    cout << sum_to_n(1) << endl; // 0

    return 0;
};
```

The `is_prime` function checks if a number is prime. If the number is less than 2, we immediately return false as numbers less than 2 are not prime. For numbers 2 and 3, we return true since they are the first two primes and would not be correctly evaluated by the loop.

Then we check if n is divisible by 2 or 3. If it is, then it can't be prime and we return false.

After that, we check if our number n is divisible by a series of numbers starting from 5 and incrementing by 6 each time. This increments the prime checking number up to √n, since no higher numbers need be checked. A number is not prime if it can be divided by a number less than or equal to its square root.

The outer for loop in `sum_to_n` function iterates over all numbers from 1 to n, checks if they are prime and if so, adds them to the sum. Finally, it returns the sum.

