It's generally recommended to use composition over inheritance in such situations.

Here's why:

1. Inheritance breaks encapsulation: If you inherit from std::vector, every method of std::vector becomes a part of your public API (unless you deliberately hide them). This can lead to unexpected behavior if the user of your class uses one of those methods in a way that your class is not prepared for.

2. Liskov Substitution Principle: If a class B is a subclass of class A, then wherever A is expected, we should be able to use an instance of B without any issues. However, this is not the case here because query2 is not just a std::vector; it has extra functionality (parsing) that std::vector does not have, so query2 can't be used wherever a std::vector is expected.

3. Inheritance locks you into a single implementation: If you decide later that std::vector is not the best containers to use for your implementation, you can't easily switch to another container if you've used inheritance.

So, composition is a better choice for this situation:

```cpp
struct Query {
