You are accessing a shared resource (finished_flag) between the main thread and the thread you've launched. Even if there is only one thread changing this value, there might be race conditions between reading and writing it and that could produce unexpected behavior in your program (e.g., a stale read). 

In general, you need to protect shared data with a mutex when multithreading to ensure thread-safety. Although in your case with only one writer and one reader it might work as you expect most of the time, there is a chance for stale reads, and this can lead to very hard to track bugs.

It's also worth mentioning that modern C++ has built-in threading libraries that you're encouraged to use rather than using boost for this job. So, instead of using a plain boolean flag, you might use an `std::atomic<bool>` which provides safe read/write operations between multiple threads without the need for mutexes and locks.

Sample C++11 code:

```cpp
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

class myclass {
public:
    std::atomic<bool> finished_flag;

    void mymethod(std::atomic<bool> *finished_flag) {
        // Do some work...
        *finished_flag = true;
    }

    void start() {
        finished_flag = false;
        std::thread t(&myclass::mymethod, this, &finished_flag);
        t.detach();   // If you want the thread to run independently.
    }
};
```
You should always assume a worst case scenario when dealing with threading to avoid potential bugs and issues in your code.

