```

The function should take the ID of the starting node as input and return a vector of strings representing the order in which the nodes are visited during the BFS traversal.

Example:
Given the following graph:
```
addNode(Node::Type::ALPHA, "A", "Node A");
addNode(Node::Type::ALPHA, "B", "Node B");
addNode(Node::Type::ALPHA, "C", "Node C");
addNode(Node::Type::ALPHA, "D", "Node D");
addEdge("A", "B");
addEdge("A", "C");
addEdge("B", "D");
```
The bfsTraversal("A") should return {"A", "B", "C", "D"} as the order of visited nodes during the BFS traversal starting from node "A".

a:
```cpp
std::vector<std::string> bfsTraversal(const std::string& startNode) {
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    std::queue<std::string> queue;
    std::set<std::string> visited;

    queue.push(startNode);
    visited.insert(startNode);

    while (!queue.empty()) {
        std::string current = queue.front();
        queue.pop();
        result.push_back(current);

        // Get the adjacent nodes of the current node
        for (auto it = visited_.begin(); it != visited_.end(); ++it) {
            if (visited.find(*it) == visited.end()) {
                queue.push(*it);
                visited.insert(*it);
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

In the solution, we use a queue to keep track of the nodes to be visited. We start with the given startNode, mark it as visited, and add it to the queue. Then, we iterate through the queue, visiting each node and adding its adjacent nodes to the queue if they have not been visited before. Finally, we return the result vector containing the order of visited nodes during the BFS traversal.

