    Eigen::Matrix<double, N, 1> result;

    std::istringstream iss(str);
    double value;
    std::size_t count = 0;
    while (iss >> value) {
        if (count >= N) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Input string contains more values than the specified vector size");
        }
        result(count, 0) = value;
        count++;
    }

    if (count != N) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Input string does not contain enough values for the specified vector size");
    }

    if (!iss.eof()) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Error parsing input string");
    }

    return result;
}
```

This solution implements the `toVectorNd` function to parse the input string and store the double values in the Eigen vector. It uses a stringstream to extract the double values and performs error-checking to ensure that the parsed values match the specified vector size and format. If any errors occur during parsing, appropriate exceptions are thrown to handle the exceptional cases.

This problem can be translated into a multidimensional knapsack problem, with the target function considering both the cost and quality of the services. Due to the fact that we have to pay attention to the quantity, we also have to take into account the quantity in our algorithms.

Here is an example program to accomplish this task with dynamic programming:

Please note that due to the limitations of the assistant the problem was simplified. For the real world problem a more sophisticated method should be carried out which considers all factors such as alignment of data, input sanitation and validation against negative values.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

class Service {
public:
    int expenditure;
    double quality;
    int quantity_needed;
    std::string particulars;

    Service(int e, double q, int n, std::string p) : expenditure(e), quality(q), quantity_needed(n), particulars(p) {}
};

int solve(std::vector<Service> &services, int budget, double min_quality) {
    int N = services.size();
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(budget + 1,std::vector<int>(N + 1));

    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= budget; ++j) {
            dp[j][i] = dp[j][i - 1];
            for (int k = 1; k <= services[i - 1].quantity_needed && k * services[i - 1].expenditure <= j; ++k) {
                int val = services[i - 1].quality * k + dp[j - k * services[i - 1].expenditure][i - 1];
                if (val > dp[j][i] && val > min_quality)
                    dp[j][i] = val;

            }
        }
    }
    return dp[budget][N];
}

int main() {
    std::vector<Service> services;
    services.push_back(Service(100, 0.75, 50, "ManuscriptPrinting"));
    services.push_back(Service(250, 0.9, 20, "ManuscriptEditing"));
    int budget = 500;
    double min_quality = 0.8;

    std::cout << "The maximum quality with budget " << budget << " and minimum quality " << min_quality << ": " << solve(services, budget, min_quality) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This program first initializes a 2D dp table where dp[i][j] represents the highest combined quality that can be achieved with the initial j budget and first i services. It then iterates the services and each possible service quantity within the service expenditure and budget. If the current quantity can bring a higher combined quality without violating the minimum quality constraint, update the dp value. Finally, return the maximum value in dp[budget].

