#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

double findMedian(std::vector<int>& nums) {
    std::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    int len = nums.size();

    if (len % 2 == 0) { //even length
        return (nums[len/2 - 1] + nums[len/2]) / 2.0;
    } 
    else { //odd length
        return nums[len/2];
    }
}
```
This function works by first sorting the array in ascending order. It then checks if the length of the array is an even number or an odd number. 

If it's an even number, the median is the average of the two middle numbers. This is achieved by adding the two middle numbers together and dividing by 2. 

If it's an odd number, the median is simply the middle number. 

Note: It use anonymous array with `std::vector<int>` data type. 

**IMPORTANT:**  It is important to note that sorting the array can have a time complexity of O(n log n). If you need to find the median in linear time complexity, you will need to use a different algorithm, like the Quickselect algorithm (a variant of the Quicksort sorting algorithm), which is beyond the scope of this query.

