Given the problem, we need to mirror the elements of the vector at non-multiple of five positions. Mirroring means reversing the order of the elements. But at positions which are multiple of five, we need to keep them as is but sorted in ascending order.

Here is the complete C++ code to solve this problem:

```c++
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;

vector<int> sort_fifth(vector<int> m){
    vector<int> sortPart, res;
    for(int i = 0; i < m.size(); ++i){
        if((i+1) % 5 == 0){
            sortPart.push_back(m[i]);
            sort(sortPart.begin(), sortPart.end());
            for(int j = sortPart.size() - 1; j >= 0; --j){
                res.push_back(sortPart[j]);
            }
            sortPart.clear();
        }else{
            sortPart.push_back(m[i]);
        }
    }
    reverse(sortPart.begin(), sortPart.end());
    for(int i = 0; i<sortPart.size(); ++i){
        res.push_back(sortPart[i]);
    }
    return res;
}
```
In this code, we use two vectors. vector `m` is the given input vector while vector `sortPart` is a temporary storage for handling multiples and non-multiples of five. For elements at positions which are multiples of five in the input vector, we add them to `sortPart`, sort it and then add the sorted elements to the resultant vector `res`, from last to first (to mirror the elements). For elements at positions which are non-multiples of five in the input vector, we simply add them to `sortPart`. After traversing the whole input vector, if there are any remaining elements in `sortPart` (these will be the elements at non-multiple of five positions at the end of the input vector), we reverse them and add to the `res` vector.

