int divSum(int n) { 
   if (n == 1)  return 1;
   int result = 1; 
   int p = least_prime[n];
   int j = n / p;
   while (j != 1) { 
      if (least_prime[j] == p) {
         result *= p;
         result += 1;
         p *= p;
      } else { 
         result *= (1 + p);
         p = least_prime[j];
      }
      j /= least_prime[j];
   }
   result *= (1 + p);
   return result - n;
}

int main() {
    leastPrimeSieve();
    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while(t--) {
        int n;
        cin >> n; 
        cout << divSum(n) << "\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This program calculates the sum of all divisors for any given number by breaking it down into its prime factors. The Sieve of Eratosthenes helps in determining the least prime number for each number up to a given number. By repeatedly dividing the number by its least prime factor and taking the sum, it calculates the sum of divisors. The function `divSum()` returns the sum of divisors for any given number `n` and the main program handles multiple test cases with multiple inputs. 

The time complexity for creating least prime sieve is O(n log log n) and for getting sum of divisors it's approximately O(n^(1/3)) so overall time complexity is well optimized for large inputs.

