In C++, you have to implement modular exponentiation manually as there is no built-in function for it. Here is a simple implementation of the same logic from your Java code:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

long long modp(long long g, long long n, long long p) {
    long long res = 1;
    g = g % p;  // Update g if it is more than or equal to p

    if (g == 0) return 0;  // In case g is divisible by p;

    while (n > 0)
    {
        // If n is odd, multiply g with result
        if (n & 1)
            res = (res * g) % p;

        // n = n/2 , g = g*g
        n = n >> 1;
        g = (g * g) % p;
    }
    return res;
}

int main() {
    cout << modp(5, 4, 497) << endl; //outputs 445
    cout << modp(7, 3, 517) << endl; //outputs 363
    cout << modp(6, 1, 311) << endl; //outputs 6
    cout << modp(2, 10, 101) << endl; //outputs 36
    cout << modp(10, 100, 701) << endl; //outputs 69

    return 0;
}
```
The `modp` function performs the modular exponentiation operation: it computes the remainder of g^n when divided by p. This operation is frequently used in the field of cryptography. It performs this computation efficiently using the binary exponentiation algorithm, and it efficiently computes large powers without causing an overflow.

