std::vector<std::string> generateSequences(int N, int alphabetSize) {
    if (N == 1) {
        return std::vector<std::string>();
    }

    std::vector<std::string> seqs;
    std::size_t base = alphabetSize;
    seqs.reserve(static_cast<std::size_t>(std::pow(base, N)));

    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < std::pow(base, N); i++) {
        std::size_t value = i;
        std::string tmp(N, 'A');
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < N; j++) {
            tmp[N - 1 - j] = 'A' + value % base;
            value = value / base;
        }
        seqs.push_back(tmp);
    }

    return seqs;
}
```

The `generateSequences` function first checks if N is 1, in which case it returns an empty vector. Otherwise, it initializes a vector `seqs` to store the generated sequences and calculates the base value using the given `alphabetSize`. It then reserves space in the vector for the expected number of sequences to avoid unnecessary reallocations.

The function then iterates through all possible values up to `base^N`, constructing each sequence by converting the current value to the corresponding characters in the alphabet. The sequences are then added to the `seqs` vector. Finally, the function returns the vector of generated sequences.

