Reconstruct the following code snippet ensuring that it utilises thread-safe constructs and incorporates measures to prevent possible race conditions. 

int x = 0;

//This task is performed by the thread
void increment(){ 
    x++; 
}

a:
Below is a C++ multithread safe implementation of the above problem. This code uses std::mutex for mutual exclusion of threads. Mutex is used to ensure that only one thread can change the value of shared data (x, in this case) at any time and thus avoids the race condition.

```cpp
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx; // mutex for critical section
int x = 0;

//This task is performed by the thread
void safe_increment(){ 
    mtx.lock();
    x++; 
    mtx.unlock();
}
```

It's recommended to use std::lock_guard when dealing with mutexes because it provides a mechanism for owning a mutex for the duration of a scoped block. The mutex gets automatically released when execution leaves the scope.

```cpp
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx; // mutex for critical section
int x = 0;

//This task is performed by the thread
void safe_increment(){ 
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    x++; 
}
```

Both of these versions are equivalent, the latter with std::lock_guard is simply more idiomatic in modern C++. In general, it's a better practice because it ensures an exception safe code and also provides a cleaner and more readable syntax.

