```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

// Function to find the longest common suffix
string findCommonSuffix(string arr[], int n) 
{ 
    if (n == 0) return "";

    // Reverse each string in array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        reverse(arr[i].begin(), arr[i].end());

    string comSuffix = arr[0]; 
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) 
    { 
        string str = arr[i]; 
        int j; 
        for (j = 0; j < min(comSuffix.size(), str.size()); j++) 
        { 
            if (comSuffix[j] != str[j]) 
                break; 
        } 
        comSuffix = comSuffix.substr(0, j); 
    } 
    // Reverse common suffix back to original orientation
    reverse(comSuffix.begin(), comSuffix.end());

    return comSuffix; 
} 

int main() 
{ 
    string arr[] = {"geeksforgeeks", "geeks", "geek", "geezer"}; 
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]); 

    cout << "The longest common suffix is: " << findCommonSuffix(arr, n) << endl; 

    string arr2[] = {"hello", "world"};
    n = sizeof(arr2)/sizeof(arr2[0]);

    cout << "The longest common suffix is: " << findCommonSuffix(arr2, n) << endl;

    string arr3[] = {"", "world"};
    n = sizeof(arr3)/sizeof(arr3[0]);

    cout << "The longest common suffix is: " << findCommonSuffix(arr3, n) << endl;

    string arr4[] = {"world$#", "world"};
    n = sizeof(arr4)/sizeof(arr4[0]);

    cout << "The longest common suffix is: " << findCommonSuffix(arr4, n) << endl;

    string arr5[] = {"world$", "world"};
    n = sizeof(arr5)/sizeof(arr5[0]);

    cout << "The longest common suffix is: " << findCommonSuffix(arr5, n) << endl;

    return 0; 
} 
```
This code now correctly finds the longest common suffix among the strings. It first reverses all the strings, then finds the longest common prefix (which is now the longest common suffix due to the reversal), and finally reverses the result back to its original orientation. I have also included more test cases to demonstrate its functionality. This code also does not use raw pointers, thereby avoiding potential memory leaks. 

Test cases in main function call findCommonSuffix function on different string arrays to check its functionality in different scenarios.

Please note that I have now changed the function to return a string, so it is now safe to use the return value. Previously, the code was attempting to return a pointer to a local variable, which is undefined behavior in C++. The findCommonSuffix function does not allocate any new memory, so there are no memory leaks. 

Also, the time complexity of this function is O(NM), where N is the number of strings and M is the maximum length of a string. It could be improved to O(N) by sorting the array of reversed strings and only comparing the first and last elements. However, this would only be faster for large inputs where M >> N. For small inputs, the overhead of sorting would make this approach slower. Since the nature of the input is not specified in the problem, we chose to implement the simpler brute force method, which has a reasonable worst case time complexity for any input.

